# Editorial review — Movement 007, chapters 44–51

## Review status and coverage

**Disposition: targeted repair, then recheck.** The movement is structurally strong, canon-faithful, age-appropriate, and ready to hand forward after one countdown correction and two line-level read-aloud fixes. The numerical primary working-range gates pass; the remaining formula drift is secondary but worth a restrained sentence-level pass if the author is already reopening the movement.

This is a **fresh-context simulated reader review in an informed editorial context**. It is not self-review and not a real human read; it is also not a blind cold read because the canon, movement brief, formula, Reader Standard, and measured metrics were supplied. Coverage is exactly eight complete files, chapters 44–51, totaling 37,869 prose words. No claim is made about unread chapters or a full-book response.

## Unscored reader response

Interest catches when Reydan pays in coppers, studies the Ironyard rather than announcing himself, and reads the losing Blade's feet. That makes him dangerous without making him cruel, and his pleasure at finally not knowing what he will face creates an unusually clean antagonist hook. The strongest sustained sequence is the chain from Ansel's painful account through his voluntary sparring round, where “declining to be interesting” becomes a usable tactic and gives Ansel a modest, humane recovery of dignity. Session nine then changes the nature of the pressure without pretending to solve it.

Interest slips slightly through parts of chapters 47–48. Several drill explanations, tallies, and the written account restate information the reader already has before delivering the next change. The repetition is functional—the movement is about deliberate practice—but the prose sometimes explains the lesson after the scene has already made it clear.

Cael matters most because his growth is ethical as well as tactical: he asks permission, learns to treat his own habits as evidence, notices what his need has cost Ansel, and accepts fear without turning it into spectacle. Ansel is the supporting character who gains the most in the least space. I expect the next event to be the Reydan bout, with the promised timing-versus-direction problem, three redirects, the scout watching, and session nine unavailable. I would continue immediately.

## Reader lenses

Scores are editorial judgments, not demographic research or release gates. They are not averaged.

