books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:111:The format: a closed session in the training hall. Panel of three — Quenna presiding, Wray, one rotating faculty seat. Cael must demonstrate "consistent, observable practice." He is not required to name his Path. He is required to show that what he does is real, repeatable, and controlled. The tension of the chapter is calibration: every capability he shows becomes institutional record, and institutional records travel. He demonstrates the Wind-adjacent evasion framework and the Iron-adjacent surface read — his two most stable fragments — at deliberately conservative deployment, and holds back the Pressure-adjacent, the Compression-adjacent, and everything about acquisition. Showing enough to pass while banking everything that matters: the skill Vell's circuit spent a year teaching him, transplanted into a room with higher stakes.
books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:186:Cael's internal movement is the chapter's real content. Directing it has never occurred to him as a possibility — the fragments *arrive*, they have always arrived, on their own schedule, like weather. The idea that the weather might have a handle on it is destabilizing precisely because of what it implies: if it can be aimed, then what happened with Lira's Wind, with Feryn, with Brom, with Reydan, was never random. And if it was never random, he is not a person things happen to. He is a person who does things — and has been, without consent, to people he cares about. He takes this to Lira alone, at night, the way he took the first disclosure to her in Book 1. "I might have been taking from you on purpose without knowing it was on purpose." Lira, steady, after a long moment: "You told me what you knew when you knew it. Keep doing that, and there's nothing to forgive." It is the Book 1 Ch16 covenant, renewed under heavier load.
books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:214:The chapter works the pattern honestly, through the Power Log: every acquisition he has ever received came under genuine engagement — Lira's real sparring, Feryn's real bout, Brom's full-intensity sessions, Reydan's climax fight. Never from the audience. Never from a demonstration. Hypothesis, written in Cael's careful hand: "Observation is necessary but not sufficient. The architecture doesn't take what's shown. It takes what's *used against me*. Stakes are part of the mechanism." He doesn't yet know if the rule is real. Part 2's climax is its test.
books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:216:*LOCKED thread touched, not advanced:* In one late session, Karis — thorough — asks about anomalies, and Cael tells her about session nine: the Tide-adjacent reading Brom's pressure-read caught, unreproduced for a year despite methodical attempts. They spend an evening on it. Karis's conclusion, frustrated and honest: "It doesn't fit your acquisition pattern. No Tide practitioner, no sustained exposure, no stakes. It shouldn't exist." Cael: "It exists." Karis: "Then something about the model is incomplete, and I can't see the missing piece from here." She marks it in her notebook with a symbol she reserves for exactly one category: *real, unexplained, keep*. It is not resolved. It is not reframed. It stays exactly as open as Book 2 left it.
books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:227:The study content: Ember Path at Iron-tier, done with the depth Book 2 gave Iron Skin. Karis's variant is control-dominant rather than output-dominant — precise ignition points, sustained heat channels, a lattice of small commitments instead of large releases. Her tell architecture is the cleanest Cael has ever charted, because she trained at a real academy and real academies train the tells *in*: standardization is legibility. His fight problem is real regardless of the experiment — her lattice punishes his evasion framework's seam worse than anything since Reydan, and Lira drills him for it without mercy.
books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:238:**The match, full choreography.** Training hall, faculty panel, formal conditions. **First exchange:** Karis's lattice does what the drills predicted — she cuts the training space into heat channels, denies his evasion angles, forces him to spend the Wind-adjacent framework faster than it can be spent. She wins the exchange going away. She is better at match intensity than any of them modeled from her demonstrations, which is itself a finding: Karis at real stakes is a different practitioner than Karis at documentation intensity. **Second exchange:** Cael adjusts — Iron-adjacent surface read to feel ignition points forming, Compression-adjacent to survive the channel he chooses to cross instead of evade. He takes real heat doing it and closes to contact range once. It costs more than it gains. His mobility is degrading. He is, by every honest read, losing.
books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:411:A final demonstration assessment closes the semester — routine now, almost restful, the way the final circuit bout in Ardenmere was restful — and the chapter notes what routine means: an institution has *procedures* for him. Boring, monthly, stamped procedures. For a boy expelled inside twenty-four hours at fourteen, boring procedure is the most exotic thing Greyvane has given him. The Power Log receives the year's closing inventory, on the page: five confirmed fragments — Wind-adjacent, Pressure-adjacent, Iron-adjacent, Compression-adjacent, Ember-adjacent — the last one carrying the field none of the others have, *Acquisition: directed*. And one anomaly, session nine, Tide-adjacent, months old now, still unreproduced, still unexplained; he turns the entry over the way he always does, gets nowhere, the way he always does, and writes beside it only: "Still open. Still real. Patience." (LOCKED — it leaves the book exactly this open.)
