books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:37:- [ ] **B6 threads honored at their correct altitude:** *The faceless faction* — reaches across the Line twice, and the book counts both: **first on paper (Ch8)** — a genuine recovery contract for a survey kit the Compact abandoned five years ago, laid on the Lowmarch board through an edge-territory broker in registry prose, which Seln flags as written-from-inside and which the crew takes anyway; its completion report travels back west (Ch16) and tells the poster exactly which crew walks into quiet ground and walks out; **then in person (Ch17–19)** — a second posting from the same broker, priced as a lure, at a site where the poster knows six practitioners will be five bodies and one anomaly; the team that waits is off-channel, nameless, and equipped for practitioners who cannot use their Paths — because the Compact *knows what quiet ground does* (plant a, above, given teeth). The knowledge progression is explicit: the first contract was recovery plus a question; the report answered it; the second contract was the trap. Still unnamed, still unlocated, still open into Book 8+. *The Compact's question about what Seln kept* — the trap is for the case as much as the boy, and Seln says so (Ch18); the case is not taken; the dread stays live. *Asset-restriction, pending jurisdiction* — the reason nobody goes west, stated once (Ch2), and the reason the trap has to come east. *Daeva's rematch / Reydan's answer* — one log line each (Ch14, Ch24), warm, unscheduled, not advanced. *Hesk* — letter beats only, via the Lowmarch courier chain (Ch4, Ch14, Ch24); his history untouched. *Vell* — one letter (Ch24). *Ephram* — one letter, from inside (Ch9), the inside friend reporting the weather. *Vastin* — one window (Ch16), three sentences, still inside, receiving a courtesy copy and writing nothing; his departure and the classified-history material are Book 8's and are not staged, hinted, or scheduled. *Havel, Ilsev, Withrow, Coss* — offstage entirely. ⚠ 2026-09-04: Ch8's posting is not a genuine recovery contract — Ch12 line 179 rules it "For a kit that was never here" — and Vell has two letter beats (inbound Ch9 line 141, outbound Ch24 line 97), not the single Ch24 letter the card allows.
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:53:**Tide Path (new Path defined this book — PROVISIONAL; consistent with B2's "Tide-adjacent" anomaly label and the bible's Book 15 "Oryn's Tide Path burns out"):** Flow architecture. Where Ember declares ignition and Iron Skin declares density, Tide declares *current*: the perception and redirection of internal energy flow — a practitioner's own and, at contact, another's. Its civil face is healing: restoring flow to damaged architecture so the body can do the rest. Its diagnostic technique is **the reading** — contact, both hands, the healer's own current run *through* the patient's architecture to feel where it pools, breaks, or runs wrong; Oryn describes it as "listening with my hands to how you're built." Limitations, weaknesses, costs (charter §1.2, stated before second use): contact range only, always; healing spends the healer's own reserve at roughly the rate of the damage repaired — Oryn cannot heal a crowd and cannot heal herself; the reading has two modes, taught in Ch7 before either is load-bearing: the *surface* reading — hands on the hurt, seconds, finds injury and the Path beneath it — and the *deep* reading — both hands, the healer's whole current run through the whole architecture, minutes, patient still and uninjured, at a mending's cost; whole-architecture knowledge is available ONLY from the deep reading; a Tide practitioner **cannot heal what she cannot read** — architecture that returns no legible current is architecture she cannot touch, which is why Cael's injuries have always healed on their own schedule under every healer he ever saw, and why she stays. Combat use exists and is rare: at contact, a Tide practitioner can *stall* another's current for a breath — a hand on a wrist and the declaration doesn't render — at brutal cost to her own reserve. Oryn has done it three times in her life and hates it. Inside quiet ground: nothing. She is a woman with a good kit and strong hands.
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:248:[unnamed] — Tide-adjacent. Duration: sustained. Integration: partial.
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:255:Two firsts, counted on the page even now, because he is who he is: the first notice whose engagement field says *clinical* — the system's grammar bending again to describe a thing that was earnest and was help; and the first fragment that does *nothing in a fight*. He counts a third and does not write it yet: ten, and the tenth is Tide, and the anomaly has been Tide-adjacent for five years. Oryn takes her hands off him, looks at them, and says the thing that ends the chapter's second movement: "Something just changed. In you. While I was in there." Cael: "Yes." Oryn: "Did I do that?" Cael, per the covenant, before the cost arrives: "No. I did. I need to tell you what I am."
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:264:The rest chapter — integration cost, the disclosure to Oryn, and the series' fourth staged birthday, the first outside any wall and the first whose gift is late. The destabilization is paid on-page per the B3 Ch14 constraint, and it is the strangest yet: the Tide-adjacent component does not *pull* like Anchor or thin the room like Shadow — it *listens*. For three days Cael perceives his own current at the edge of every thought, the nine other fragments audible to him as flows for the first time, and the sensation of being inside a room he has lived in for eighteen years with the lights suddenly on. Karis documents the comparative data, voice back and hoarse: tenth acquisition, first non-combat, first clinical; the recovery arithmetic holding; "for now" entered at her insistence, again.
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:270:*Close on:* the inventory in the satchel, by firelight: **ten confirmed fragments** — Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide — deployment notes current; and the anomaly, session nine, Tide-adjacent, turned over with a Tide-adjacent fragment in hand for the first time, and the standing three words *retired* on the page and the reason given: "Still open. Still real. *Now I can see it.* That's worse. The tenth fragment lets me feel my own currents, and there's one in the place session nine logged, and it isn't the tenth, and it isn't any of the nine. It was there before Oryn. It was there before Brom's read caught it. I don't know what it is. I know where it is now. Patience is over; observation begins." One log line for the old promises: "Daeva's rematch, Reydan's answer — both ride east. Nineteen. Ten fragments. Hesk's package is on a ferry somewhere. The last one said mind the shape. I'm starting to."
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:365:**The fight, in water, in the Quiet:** **First:** the wyrm comes up under Brom — because Brom is the heaviest thing on the floor and he *stopped* to give Karis a shoulder up the wall — and the unit learns its shape in pieces: a blind, armored, jawed length of something, and the strike lands exactly where the rule said it would. **Second:** the hall as terrain — the walls are shoulder-high cover and shoulder-high *trap*; the water is knee-deep and then, at the hall's center where the floor broke, deep; and the wyrm cannot leave the water and cannot see. Seln reads it in one exchange: "It's blind. It reads the floor. Get *off* the floor." The walls. Six people on a wall-top, one-armed, one-shouldered, and the wyrm circling the flooded hall beneath them, and Karis *copying the carvings from the wall she is standing on* because she will not get another chance, and the book lets that be funny for exactly one paragraph. **Third — the cost:** Oryn, who is not a fighter and has never once been made one, goes into the water — because Brom's shoulder has opened and he is on the wrong wall and slipping, and she is the only one close, and the chapter refuses to make it heroic: she is a healer who cannot heal here reaching a patient who is falling, and she does the one thing the afternoon taught — she does not stop; she keeps moving, skipping-stone, and the wyrm's first pass goes under her and misses, and the second will not. **Fourth — the turn:** Cael, off the wall, into the water, *declaring* — and the decision is the afternoon's rule turned into a weapon with two fragments the reader has watched work since Book 2 and Book 6: he plants. Compression-adjacent to take the first strike on himself instead of on her, and then Anchor-adjacent — the fixed-point binding taken from the nameless specialist on the Ostrand road — laid *on the floor*, one fixed point of held pressure at the hall's broken center, the one thing on the floor that is not moving and will not move, heavier to the wyrm's sense than any body in the water. A thing that hunts what stays commits to the thing that stays hardest. It surfaces at the fixed point, whole for the first time in anyone's memory, jaws on stone that does not give — and Brom, from the wall, one-shouldered, puts his weight where Cael tells him and Seln and Lira put two bolts each into the pale underside the strike has exposed, at contact range, where nothing armored is. The wyrm does not die. It lets go of a floor that has stopped answering and goes back under the spring and does not come up. The unit leaves it there. Nobody wants the walls that badly, and the walls are copied. Win-sentence, writable: *Cael took away the wyrm's ability to choose its target by giving the floor one point that never moved.* The tenth fragment is used in no fight in this book; its first use on anything but Cael and Oryn is Chapter 22's, and it is refused.
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:378:**The first, on himself, alone, at night** — and the book's discipline is absolute: what he finds is described as shape and sensation and never as explanation. Ten currents, the way Oryn said: he cannot find the banks either, cannot find where one flow stops and the next begins, and the not-finding is described as what it is — a limit of the reading, his and hers — and not as a fact about the architecture. And the eleventh thing — the place session nine logged, five years ago, on a training floor in Ardenmere with no Tide practitioner in the city: not a current. A *stillness*. Something at the center of the architecture that does not flow and is not empty, that the ten currents move *around* the way water moves around a stone it has always known was there. He sits with it for an hour. He does not name it. He does not theorize. The log, in full: "Read myself. Ten currents, no banks — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A *still place*, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience." (LOCKED — the anomaly deepened, located, unexplained; the Fractured Path's true nature is Book 13's; nothing here approaches *primordial*, *source*, or *before*.)
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:380:**The second, on Oryn, with her consent, at her direction** — the clinical exchange completed in the other direction: he tells her, exactly, what he found. She listens the way she listens to a patient describe a pain, and her answer is the healer's, and the book's honest one: "A still place that everything goes around. I've never felt that in anyone." Cael: "Is it wrong?" Oryn: "I don't know what right would look like in you. Nobody does. That's not a comfort; it's a finding. Write it down and stop touching it. Things you keep touching don't heal." He writes it down.
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:397:*Close on:* the log, on the ridge, the fourth circle below in the dark: "Four sites. One line. The last one's twice the size and the Compact fenced it. The bearing runs through it and keeps going. Karis says four points is an argument. I say it's a bearing — the kind their maps draw as nothing. Home is Lowmarch now. I just wrote that without noticing. Nineteen, ten fragments, a still place at the center of me, and a line on a map that somebody drew before there were maps. We're not done. We're *oriented*."
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:406:The ledger of the year, in its established forms. The Power Log inventory, in Hesk's satchel, by an inn's fire, shown complete: **ten confirmed fragments** — Wind-adjacent (Lira), Pressure-adjacent (Feryn), Iron-adjacent (Brom), Compression-adjacent (Reydan), Ember-adjacent (Karis), Shadow-adjacent (Seln), Storm-adjacent (Daeva), Anchor-adjacent (unnamed), Blade-adjacent (Ephram), Tide-adjacent (Oryn) — ten integrated abilities, deployment notes current, the tenth carrying the field none of the others carry, *clinical*; and the anomaly, session nine, no longer an anomaly *entry* but a *location*: "The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues." One log line for the old promises: "Reydan will get his answer. Daeva will get her rematch. Both are going to need a map." Letters, on the last ferry: Hesk's package, at last, six weeks late — instrument-maker's work, a *straightedge*, brass, folding, calibrated in a hand Cael knows, and the note in full: *"Nineteen. I'm told it's late; the ferry's fault, not mine. Your grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. This one's true to a hair. Mind the shape. — H."* Cael looks at Karis's map, and at the straightedge, and does not say anything, and the chapter does not need him to. To Hesk, west: *"Nineteen, past the Line, and there's a roof. It's an inn. The shape you said to mind is a line, and I've got the tool for it now. — C."* To Vell, because her ledger holds his first honest record and Pike's holds his newest, and she will want to know there is a second ledger-keeper on the continent who stopped numbering him.
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:423:- [x] **SECRET discipline — none discharged, all sealed:** The Quieting's SOURCE (the Architect's preserved will priming a reset) is untouched — no character theorizes a maker, a purpose, an intelligence, or a spread; every site is old and stable; the word *Architect* appears nowhere; the book's largest fact (Cael unaffected; the Compact measured and filed) is stated as fact and explicitly not interpreted (Karis's unentered hypothesis, Ch15–16; Cael's "I'm not going to guess," Ch22). The Fractured Path's true nature (Book 13) is untouched — what the reading finds in Cael is *shape* ("currents without banks," "a still place everything goes around"), never *source*, *primordial*, or *before*; Oryn offers no theory of what he is made of (E01 repair: her only claim is that nothing in him is *shaped like a Path*, which the drafted books have said since Book 1). The Book 3 SECRET (the mechanism) expands by exactly ONE person: **Oryn learns it in Ch13–14**, the circle's third expansion (Karis B3, Seln B6, Oryn B7) — six people; Teague does not learn it (Ch22 explicitly); Pike does not; the off-channel team does not (they saw a practitioner declare on the rim; they did not see him declare *inside*, which is the fact that matters and the fact they lack — stated in Ch19). Book 8's Architect SECRET: untouched — strata language keeps the floors "older than the word they retired" (Ch15) and stops there. ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:428:- [x] **Fragment count at book close:** 10 confirmed integrations — Wind-adjacent (B1, Lira), Pressure-adjacent (B1, Feryn), Iron-adjacent (B2 Ch13, Brom), Compression-adjacent (B2 Ch24, Reydan), Ember-adjacent (B3 Ch13, Karis), Shadow-adjacent (B4 Ch13, Seln), Storm-adjacent (B5 Ch21, Daeva), Anchor-adjacent (B6 Ch13, unnamed), Blade-adjacent (B6 Ch20, Ephram), **Tide-adjacent (B7 Ch13, Oryn — first non-combat-use fragment, first *clinical* engagement, first Iron-equivalent notice since Blade)**. Plus the anomaly (B2 Ch19) — CONVERGED, NOT RESOLVED: located by the tenth fragment as a "still place" at the architecture's center (Ch14, Ch22, Ch24), explicitly NOT the tenth fragment, NOT the Quieting, NOT any of the nine; the standing three words retired on the page for stated reasons (Ch14); never counted as an eleventh entry; its resolution remains reserved far past Arc 2. Bible trajectory check: 22 abilities by Book 10 (age 23) leaves twelve across Books 8–9 and the B10 skip — Book 8 architecture should plan an accelerated acquisition rate and say so. ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:433:- [x] **LOCKED threads verified untouched or correctly maintained:** Tide-adjacent anomaly — converged, located, deepened, NOT resolved (see fragment count); the book explicitly separates it from the Quieting (Ch22: "It isn't the Quiet") and from the tenth fragment (Ch14). [UNBOUND] — ONE controlled appearance (Ch15), as a stratum limit in Karis's method-voice; no meaning advanced. Daeva's rematch, Reydan's answer — one log line each (Ch14, Ch24), warm, not advanced. Iron Skin watcher, market stranger, Hesk's history (letters only), Vell's session (letter only), Coss's grade — untouched. Seln's cache — carried, unopened, targeted, kept. Withrow's ledger, Havel's transfer, Ilsev's after — untouched. NEW open threads created deliberately: the fourth site (B8's on-ramp), Oryn's route (pressure), the reading turned outward (the standing moral stake, paid once, re-armed), the still place (the anomaly's new form), Teague's crew (spring), and the Compact's demonstrated awareness of quiet ground (dread, with a fence around it). ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:435:- [x] **The internal arc staged honestly at both poles:** *No instrument out here reads me* written in the log, in relief, Chapter 2; lived in Ch5's freedom chapter; cracked at the stone, Ch9 ("Mine didn't"); landed intellectually at the wall with Lira, Ch16 ("the only thing that worked"); located in the body, Ch22 ("a still place everything goes around"); landed finally over the map, Ch24 — *nothing out here reads me, and the oldest thing out here stops every Path but mine*. The book's discipline: the freedom is real and stays real (Pike's ledger never once asks for paper; the last chapter's "home is Lowmarch" is earned) — the complication is not that the edge territories measure him after all, but that something older than measurement paced these floors first, every Path in the party fails inside them, his does not, and nobody — including him — is permitted a *why* on the page. Arc 3's promise delivered whole in Part 1 before it is complicated in Parts 2–3, per the bible's sentence. ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-02.md:53:The lecture wing was the newest structure on the grounds by a wide margin, and it looked it — clean-cut stone rather than the weathered coursing the wall and the training hall shared, the only building on the whole complex that seemed to have been built with Greyvane itself in mind rather than inherited from whatever the waystation had needed centuries before anyone had thought to call this place an academy. Three floors of classrooms, and the clerk explained the organizing principle with the flat pride of someone reciting a fact she considered obviously correct: the rooms were sorted by Path family, not by tier. A Wind-adjacent lecture sat beside a Tide-adjacent one on the same floor, regardless of whether the students inside were Copper or Iron or, in the theoretical upper reaches nobody at Greyvane had much cause to teach, Silver. Cael worked at this as they climbed the stairwell. Most academies he'd read about — and he'd read about a great many, in the months before this offer had arrived — sorted by tier, because tier was the axis institutions actually cared about ranking students along. Sorting by Path family instead suggested an institution more interested in what a student could eventually understand than in where they currently stood, and he thought that told him something true about Naveth's clerical-error philosophy that the man himself hadn't quite said out loud.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-04.md:31:The binder told him what "enough" could safely mean. Four fragments, current: Wind-adjacent, Pressure-adjacent, Iron-adjacent, Compression-adjacent. Of the four, two had a shape stable enough to survive being shown to strangers who would write down what they saw. The Wind-adjacent evasion framework — a year and a half now since Lira had first taught it to him without either of them understanding what it was, drilled since through every circuit bout that had come after until it had stopped being a borrowed thing and become simply a thing he did, the way breathing was a thing he did. And the Iron-adjacent surface read — Brom's gift, quieter, a pressure-sense at the skin that told him where an opponent's weight was about to go a half-beat before it went there. Both had been shown, in cruder forms, on the Ardenmere circuit, in front of judges and crowds and at least one Compact-adjacent official. Neither was, at this point, a secret worth protecting. They were simply skill. Vell's circuit had spent the better part of a year teaching him to make that skill legible to people who were watching for exactly this — and now he was going to spend the lesson in a room with far higher stakes than a circuit bout had ever carried. He paused over the page there, crossed out the grander word he'd almost written, and replaced it, deliberately, with something plainer. Higher stakes than a circuit bout. That was the whole sentence. It didn't need decorating.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-04.md:33:The Pressure-adjacent stayed in the binder. So did the Compression-adjacent, newer, less understood, and — more importantly — so did every fact about *acquisition* itself: how the fragments arrived, what triggered them, what the binder's growing collection of entries actually represented. That was not a demonstration. That was the entire architecture underneath the demonstration, and no panel, however carefully constituted, was getting a look at the foundation when all they'd asked to see was the house.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-04.md:67:He kept the deployment small at first — not the full circuit-bout intensity he'd used against Reydan in the last exchange of his life at Ardenmere, but a controlled, almost pedagogical version of it, moving through a sequence of evasion patterns at exactly the pace Lira had built for him: fast enough to make the framework real, slow enough that nothing about the exchange would read as combat rather than demonstration. The partner struck on Wray's count — committed, honest strikes, nothing theatrical — and Cael let the panel watch him read each one a half-beat early, adjust his stance a fraction before the strike arrived rather than after, close distance at angles that should have been impossible to anticipate and weren't. Between the second and third pass, Wray raised a finger, changed the count's rhythm without warning, and watched what happened to him when the pattern he'd been fed collapsed. What happened was the framework, doing what it did: the read came off the partner's body, not the count, and the third pass landed identical to the first two. Wray lowered the finger and wrote something. He did it four times in all, identical in structure, varied only in the angle of attack, because *consistent* was the word doing the actual work in the provision's language and he intended to satisfy it beyond any possible dispute.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-04.md:77:He held everything else back with the same discipline he'd once used to keep two fragments quiet in front of Coss's questions in a market square over a year and a continent of growing up ago. No Pressure-adjacent. No Compression-adjacent. Nothing about how any of it arrived in him in the first place. The panel had asked to see a house. He showed them a house, furnished carefully, with the foundation nowhere in view.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-01.md:9:Cael had spent most of the walk doing what he always did with unstructured time, which was fail to leave it unstructured. He'd gone through the binder twice — four fragment entries, the deployment-range column current, the Tide-adjacent anomaly still sitting in its own section with nothing under *Note* but a single line he'd written and not crossed out: *Session nine. Could not reproduce. Still don't know what that was.* He'd read Hesk's last letter three times, which was one more than he usually allowed himself, on the theory that letters were meant to be received once and carried rather than worn down through repeated handling. He'd made it three times anyway.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-01.md:11:The Tide-adjacent entry was the one his eyes kept returning to, though, more than the letter, more than anything else in the binder. Four confirmed fragments sat in their own tidy rows, deployment range noted, source noted, everything a practitioner could reasonably want to know about a thing he now understood well enough to use without thinking about it. The fifth entry had none of that. Just the date, the bare fact of what had happened in Brom's sparring circle that one afternoon, and the single unfinished line underneath it. He had turned it over so many times in the months since that turning it over no longer felt like investigation. It felt like checking that a scar was still there — a habit rather than an inquiry, done less because he expected to learn anything new and more because stopping felt like admitting he'd given up on it.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-12.md:57:His own fight problem, entirely separate from the second experiment running underneath it, was real and immediate: her lattice sat directly on top of the seam in his evasion framework, the same seam that had cost him ground against the hardest opponent he'd faced on the circuit — the gap between committing to a redirect and completing it, the half-beat where his Wind-adjacent framework had to choose a direction before it could execute one. Karis's lattice didn't attack that seam once, the way a single powerful opponent might. It sat on top of it continuously, a grid of small threats that made every redirect a bet against a hundred tiny commitments instead of one large one. Against Reydan, the seam had been a door an opponent had to find. Against the lattice, it was a door standing open in every direction at once.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-11.md:15:"Because the alternative is waiting for it to happen to someone by accident again, the way it happened with Lira, with Feryn, with Brom, with Reydan." She did not flinch delivering the list, which told him she had done her homework past what the terms technically allowed her to know. "None of them consented to being a source in advance. I can. That's not nothing, Cael. That might be the only ethically clean version of this experiment anyone is ever going to be able to run."
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-11.md:49:He watched her run the same declaration eleven times, mapping the ignition timing down to what felt like a quarter-second of consistency, holding his attention on her with the same total focus he'd once held on Reydan's third exchange, on Feryn's shoulder, on every moment that had ever produced something. Nothing arrived. No texture. No pre-acquisition sense of a space opening. No notice. Just a boy standing in an empty room, watching a girl light small precise fires, wanting.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-11.md:83:Lira's Wind-adjacent had come during a real bout, back in Ardenmere — and he made himself reconstruct it honestly, not the summary but the moment: her evasion patterns deployed against him at genuine speed, the yard's packed dirt under his feet, the hot embarrassment of being outclassed and the hotter need not to be, a sparring session that had mattered, that had cost something every time he lost it badly. Feryn's Pressure-adjacent had come from a real fight, fourth exchange, a shoulder against his that had carried actual weight behind it, in a yard where nobody was pretending. Brom's Iron-adjacent had come from full-intensity training, the sessions where Brom had stopped being careful with him because Cael had asked him to stop, where getting a read wrong meant taking a hit that echoed in his bones for a day. Reydan's Compression-adjacent had come from the climax of a circuit match with a scout in the crowd and everything he'd built riding on the outcome — had come, he could admit now, at very nearly the last moment it could have arrived and still saved him.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-11.md:105:"There's an anomaly. A session with Brom, months ago now. A single reading, Tide-adjacent, that came through his pressure-read for less than a second and then never repeated. I've tried to reproduce it a dozen times since. Nothing."
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-11.md:123:"We keep it." She opened her notebook again, flipped to a page near the back that he hadn't seen before, and made a small mark beside the single line she'd written about session nine — a symbol he didn't recognize, precise, deliberate, clearly not decorative. "I have exactly one category in this entire notebook that gets this mark. It means: real, unexplained, keep. It's not a failure state. It's not a hypothesis waiting to be confirmed. It's a fact I don't understand yet, filed honestly as a fact I don't understand yet, instead of forced into an explanation that would make me feel better and teach me nothing true."
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-11.md:125:He looked at the mark for a long moment. It was, he thought, the most honest thing anyone had ever done with the anomaly — not explaining it away, not dismissing it, not pretending three weeks of careful experimentation had somehow accounted for it. Just marking it as real and leaving it alone. He had carried session nine for months the way you carried a stone whose weight nobody else would credit. Watching her enter it into the most disciplined notebook he'd ever seen, under the most exacting category that notebook contained, was — he searched for the honest word and found it — validating. Not answered. Witnessed. There was a difference, and he was old enough now to know which one was actually on offer, and to take it.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-11.md:137:*The Tide-adjacent anomaly remains exactly as unexplained as it was the night it happened. Karis marked it "real, unexplained, keep" — the only entry in her notebook with that classification. I find I trust that mark more than I would trust an explanation. An explanation this early would almost certainly be wrong. The mark, at least, is honest.*
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-14.md:55:He'd written it down before breakfast, dated, timed, with the care he gave every entry that mattered. *Fragment behavior destabilized approximately eighteen hours post-directed-acquisition. Wind-adjacent misfired twice in morning training; resolved by session's end. Iron-, Pressure-, Compression-adjacent: answering normally, checked individually. Ember-adjacent: present, partial, untested — not touching it until the others settle. Cause: unknown. Suspected: the interior action required to direct an acquisition draws on the same architecture that holds the others steady, and asking it to do something new costs something borrowed from what it already does.*
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-14.md:67:He had taken pieces of four people before this and never once felt the person in the piece — or never let himself look. He was looking now. He didn't know yet what it meant that he could see her in it. He suspected, uneasily, that it had always been true of all of them — that some part of Lira's speed and Feryn's weight and Brom's patience and Reydan's ferocity had come along inside the fragments, riding under the capability like a watermark, and that he had spent a year and a half carrying his people with him in a sense far more literal than the sentence usually meant.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-14.md:109:He made himself write out what he actually knew, in columns, because columns disciplined a frightened mind better than prose did. Known: directed acquisition was possible, once, under conditions of sustained observation plus genuine stakes. Known: it carried a destabilization cost to the existing fragments, duration roughly a day, severity two misfires, this time. Known: the panel had seen the effect and not the mechanism, and the record was drafted, and the record would hold unless he handed someone a reason to reopen it. Unknown: whether he could refuse the reach if the conditions assembled themselves again without his consent — in a real fight, against a real enemy, in front of exactly the witnesses Quenna feared. Unknown: whether the cost scaled, and along what axis, and whether some future taking would ask a price the older fragments couldn't pay. Unknown, last and largest, written slowly: what the mechanism was *for*. A thing with a handle was a tool. Tools had purposes. Somebody's purposes. He looked at that line longer than any of the others, then drew a small box around it, which was his private notation for *not yet* — the notation he'd once used for the Tide-adjacent anomaly, and for the older word in Vell's archive, and for every door he'd found before he'd found its key.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-06.md:105:Cael ran his evasion framework — the Wind-adjacent movement patterns that had carried him through most of a year and a half of circuit work, the ones so deeply worn into his body by now that he barely had to think through them consciously anymore. He ran the whole sequence three times through: the slow version first, joints loose, each transition exaggerated so the underlying shape stood out the way a diagram stood out from a photograph; then the working version, the one he'd actually used against Reydan and a dozen circuit opponents before him, fast enough that a spectator without training would only register motion rather than method; then a third pass, slower again, at Quenna's request from the section's edge, so Karis could watch the same architecture at two speeds and note where it changed and where it didn't.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-24.md:29:*Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Force absorption, redirect. Earned in the worst fight I'd survived up to that night, a year and a season back now, against the only opponent who ever told me to find him later. Someday I intend to. This entry is the reminder.*
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-24.md:41:*Anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Unreproduced. Unexplained.*
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-13.md:3:The morning of the match, Cael woke before the bell and lay still for a while in the grey, taking inventory the way he did before anything that mattered — hands, breath, the four settled weights of the fragments where they lived, each one answering when he turned his attention to it, the way banked coals answered a poker. Wind-adjacent, recovered from the week's drills. Iron-adjacent, steady. Pressure-adjacent, steady. Compression-adjacent, steady. And the fifth space, the one that wasn't a space, the one he had spent three weeks learning the exact shape of by failing to fill it. He did not reach for it. Karis's coaching had been unambiguous on the point, and he had decided, somewhere in the night, to obey it with the totality he usually reserved for Vell's rules: from the moment he left this bed, there was no experiment. There was a match against the best formally trained opponent he had ever faced, and he was, by every sober accounting, likely to lose it, and his entire job was to make losing it as expensive as possible.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-13.md:55:He brought the Compression-adjacent framework in behind the read, not to evade the channels this time but to survive crossing one directly, taking the heat rather than dodging it, because dodging had already proven too slow. He picked the channel himself — the shortest seam, the one her geometry least expected to be challenged, because no sane fighter crossed fire to stand closer to the person making it — hardened everything the Compression fragment knew how to harden, and went through.
books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-13.md:123:He read it four times, sitting on the training hall floor with the panel still talking somewhere behind him and Karis still catching her breath a few feet away, because four fragments across a year and a half had never once carried that field, and he had genuinely, honestly, not known until this exact moment that the field existed at all. Every notice he had ever received had ended at the note line. This one had a fifth line, appended in the same flat administrative voice as all the rest, as though the system had been holding the field in reserve the entire time — as though somewhere in whatever ledger the notices came from, there had always been a column for this, sitting empty through Lira and Feryn and Brom and Reydan, waiting for the first entry that qualified. Not a name for the fragment. A name for the *act* — the system itself, in its flat administrative language, confirming that this one, unlike every other, had been chosen.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-13.md:229:[unnamed] — Tide-adjacent. Duration: sustained. Integration: partial.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-13.md:242:Ten. The tenth was Tide. The log had had a word in it for five years, from a session on a floor in Ardenmere with no Tide practitioner in the city, and the word was *Tide-adjacent*, and it had sat under three other words that said *patience*, and now there was a fragment with the same word on it, a real one, with a source, with hands. He did not know what that meant. He did not let himself put a verb after it. He filed it where it would keep.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-22.md:39:He knew what he could say, and he counted it, because counting was the thing he had brought back to the marsh's edge at the end of the hour and let out of its stillness. It had been there before Oryn; session nine was five years old and she was six weeks, near enough. It had been there before Brom's read caught it, because a read catches what is already there. It had been there before the ring; he had walked into a floor where every Path in the party died and it had not done anything, because it did not do anything, and he had not felt it then because he had not had a way to feel it, and now he did, and it was the same. And it was not the Quiet. The Quiet was outside him — a line on the ground, two hundred meters from a floor, that stopped Paths at a stride — and this was inside him, and it stopped nothing; the fragments went round it as if they had always gone round it, which, as far back as he could feel, they had.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-22.md:41:He took his hands off himself, and felt the shape of the reading go, from his own side, and sat at the marsh's edge in the dark with the ten ringing faint in him and the still place at the middle of them not ringing at all, and wrote it by the fire's edge, in the plain hand, an hour later, when his hands had stopped being a healer's and had gone back to being his.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-22.md:45:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-22.md:67:Oryn said: "A still place that everything goes around. I've never felt that in anyone."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-22.md:125:*Third reading done, before dawn. Told Oryn, at noon, all of it, exactly, with my hand on her wrist and her leave to have it there. She said: a still place that everything goes around, and she's never felt it in anyone, and that's a finding and not a comfort, and write it down and stop touching it. Written. Not touched since. It's there. It'll be there. Observation continues; the pencil doesn't have to.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-21.md:153:He took it. Reydan's, at contact — the jaw's strike arriving through the water into a frame that did not give, the force going through him and out along his own bones into a floor that was fused stone and gave nothing back, and the water going up round him in a sheet, and the length of the thing against his legs and his chest for the length of a breath, armored, cold — and he was still standing, in water to the waist, at the broken edge, and it had struck a thing that stayed and had not moved it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-01.md:9:*Inventory, because I do one at the start of anything. I am eighteen. Nine confirmed: Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Storm, Anchor, Blade, and Shadow, which costs from waking whether I use it or not and drifted twice today on a road with nobody on it. Deployment since the Line: none. One anomaly. Session nine. Still open. Still real. Patience.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:145:He let it off its hold in the stride before the stride, so that when his right foot came down on the road's side of the stone the burst was already in him and going, and he landed on the rim's ground a foot from where a hooded figure with a crossbow had turned toward a sound that had not been possible a breath before — twelve paces of road in the time a breath takes, the scrub going flat and white beside him, the loudest thing that had ever come out of that bowl on purpose. Storm with it. Daeva's, the pressure-differential seeded ahead of him along the rut so that the air went wrong for ten paces and the man's shot, when it came, went where the air said and not where the man did. Compression at contact, Reydan's, as the man's shoulder came round with the stock — the force taken through him and out along his own frame into the road, and the man's arm going the way a man's arm goes when the thing he hit was not there. Ember, Karis's, one point of white held out at arm's length, lighting the rim for five paces round, so that the other five on the rim, looking across the bowl for a declaration inside it, found one *outside* it, on their own ground, at contact with one of them, in a suite that four books of public floors had made the most recognizable set of architectures on the continent short of a Gold-tier woman's own.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md:27:Lira, one-armed, on the taped foot, walking as she had walked since the Ring — the arm across her chest, the good hand free, the stride not one hair shorter for it — and carrying Oryn's crossbow across her back on its strap, which she had not given back and Oryn had not asked for. Brom with the kit on his good shoulder and the middle box on the same one and the other carried still and forward, and no word about it, and Oryn's eyes on it at every halt. Karis with the map in her coat and the straightedge in her hand, open, because she had opened it and had not found a reason to close it. Seln ahead, always ahead, with the case on his back and the ledger inside his coat, reading a road nobody had walked for a season. Oryn with her mule's rope in her left hand and her right hand out of the coat, at every halt, as if a hurt might arrive at the fire and need it. And himself, with the ribs that had paid for the Hall saying so at every halt now that the cold had them, and a healer's two days on them gone to four, and ten things held to nothing and a still place at the middle of them that he did not touch, because a healer had said *stop touching it* and a man who had been told that by the only person who could read him did as he was told.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md:139:*Four sites. One line. The last one's twice the size and the Compact fenced it. The bearing runs through it and keeps going. Karis says four points is an argument. I say it's a bearing — the kind their maps draw as nothing. Home is Lowmarch now. I just wrote that without noticing. Nineteen, ten fragments, a still place at the center of me, and a line on a map that somebody drew before there were maps. We're not done. We're oriented.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-11.md:149:The second burst was the turn, west, back into the middle of eight animals that had followed him east, and the hound came inside the lock, low, at the leg, and he took it on the shoulder instead — Compression-adjacent, Reydan's, at contact, the animal's force going through him and out along his own frame into the ground — and it went over him and was up, and he was up, and it turned with the rest and came on.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:209:*Inventory, because I do one. Nine confirmed. One anomaly, session nine, still open, still real, patience. Deployment since the Line: none. Shadow tried again at dusk coming up from the landing, and I let it try, and it stopped. Anchor wants this doorway; it has wanted every doorway since the Ostrand road; I am letting it want. Eighteen. No injuries. Eighteen marks earned and about ten left after the jug.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:11:"Tide-adjacent," Oryn said. It was the first thing she had said in twenty-one minutes, and it was a word she was trying for weight.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:185:Then he went to the place session nine had logged.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:195:He knew three things about it that were not guesses, and he counted them. It had been there before Oryn; session nine was five years old and she was two months. It had been there before Brom's read caught it, because a read catches what is already there. And it was not the Quiet — the Quiet was outside him, a line on the ground that stopped Paths, and this was inside, and the fragments went round it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:205:*Inventory. Ten confirmed. Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected; the seal holds out here as it held inside; used once past the Line, on a floor, in front of the circle and nobody else. Storm-adjacent — Daeva; resettled once this week round the new neighbour, on the second morning, in the yard, and I heard it do it. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; it wanted the stake and it can want. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only non-hostile in the log until three days ago. Tide-adjacent — Oryn. Partial. Iron. Self and contact. Engagement: clinical. Does nothing in a fight, and is the first thing I've ever owned that lets me hear the other nine. Cost: three days of listening; the idle state learned by the third night, and it isn't quiet, it's just that I've stopped putting my hand to every string. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:211:*The anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Turned over — for the first time in five years, with a Tide-adjacent fragment in hand to turn it over with. The three words are retired. They were the right three for a thing I couldn't see and could only wait on; I've had them at the foot of every inventory since Ardenmere, and they've done their work, and I'm taking them out tonight for a reason, so here's the reason.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:213:*Still open. Still real. Now I can see it. That's worse. The tenth fragment lets me feel my own currents, and there's one in the place session nine logged, and it isn't the tenth, and it isn't any of the nine. It was there before Oryn. It was there before Brom's read caught it. I don't know what it is. I know where it is now. Patience is over; observation begins.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:215:*Daeva's rematch, Reydan's answer — both ride east. Nineteen. Ten fragments. Hesk's package is on a ferry somewhere. The last one said mind the shape. I'm starting to.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:9:He catalogued the five of them at the landing as he had catalogued them at every halt of the year, because it was the last halt and he wanted the count. Lira, one-armed, the sling gone grey with five weeks of road, the taped foot put down flat in the snow, Oryn's crossbow across her back on its strap; she had walked six days from the fourth site on that foot and had not once said the word *ankle*, and would not until a healer said the other word first. Brom, with the kit on one shoulder and the middle box on the same one, and the other shoulder carried in the same guarded set it had kept since a hall — still, and slightly forward — though the healer of no Path at Teague's fire had said it wanted binding and not hands, and it had been bound, and had healed under the binding as a shoulder heals when it is left alone by a man who cannot leave things alone. Karis with the three copies rolled under her arm in oilcloth against the snow, the ledger inside her coat where Seln had let her carry it for the last two days, and the straightedge in her hand, because she had not once put it back in the box since the marsh. Seln with the case on his back and his eyes on the landing door, counting who came out of it. And himself, with nothing in his hands, and ten things held to nothing, and the ribs quiet again since the marsh, and a still place at the middle of them that he had read three times and had not touched since, because a healer had said *stop* and he had stopped.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:67:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:69:*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:71:*Reydan will get his answer. Daeva will get her rematch. Both are going to need a map.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:81:Compression wanted him. He felt it want, where it had been wanting since Reydan: take the force, turn it, walk away from it. Anchor wanted the cart, a fixed point under the axle, the cart nailed to the mountain and the boxes with it. He had a hand's breadth of a second to price both. A declaration under a herd that went from declarations, at the one place on the bench where the split was still holding, and the split was Karis's; it stood on her line and nothing else. One more loud thing under the second half, here, and it turned again, and the fin was what it turned toward.

