===== books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-01.md =====
# Chapter 1 — The Gate

The road did most of the work of arriving before Greyvane itself did.

Three days out of Ardenmere, walking a quiet the three of them had gotten good at not needing to fill, the country changed by degrees — the flat market towns giving way to hill country, the road narrowing and steepening and then, on the third morning, doing the thing roads did before something waited at the top of them: straightening out, as if it had decided not to waste anyone's last half-mile on unnecessary turns.

The first day had been mostly farmland, open, tended country that made a person feel watched by nothing but crows, and they'd covered ground quickly on the strength of habit alone — a year and a half of circuit travel had taught Cael and Lira the same walking pace, and Brom had matched it inside a week of falling in with them, without any of them ever discussing it. The second day the farmland thinned into scrub and low woodland, and the road started climbing in earnest, gentle at first and then less so, and by evening they'd made camp in a stand of pine that smelled like every other stand of pine any of them had ever slept under, which was a comfort of its own. Cael had lain awake a while that second night listening to Brom's breathing settle and Lira shift once, twice, into a position she'd hold until morning, and had thought, not for the first time, that the sound of two other people sleeping nearby had become something close to a requirement for his own sleep, without his ever quite noticing the day it started being true.

Cael had spent most of the walk doing what he always did with unstructured time, which was fail to leave it unstructured. He'd gone through the binder twice — four fragment entries, the deployment-range column current, the Tide-adjacent anomaly still sitting in its own section with nothing under *Note* but a single line he'd written and not crossed out: *Session nine. Could not reproduce. Still don't know what that was.* He'd read Hesk's last letter three times, which was one more than he usually allowed himself, on the theory that letters were meant to be received once and carried rather than worn down through repeated handling. He'd made it three times anyway.

The Tide-adjacent entry was the one his eyes kept returning to, though, more than the letter, more than anything else in the binder. Four confirmed fragments sat in their own tidy rows, deployment range noted, source noted, everything a practitioner could reasonably want to know about a thing he now understood well enough to use without thinking about it. The fifth entry had none of that. Just the date, the bare fact of what had happened in Brom's sparring circle that one afternoon, and the single unfinished line underneath it. He had turned it over so many times in the months since that turning it over no longer felt like investigation. It felt like checking that a scar was still there — a habit rather than an inquiry, done less because he expected to learn anything new and more because stopping felt like admitting he'd given up on it.

"You're doing the thing," Lira said, on the second afternoon, not looking up from her own pace, which had settled somewhere between purposeful and impatient since the moment Ardenmere's gate had shrunk out of sight behind them.

"What thing."

"The thing where you read something you've already memorized because reading it is easier than sitting with whatever you're actually thinking about."

"I don't have that thing."

"You have a whole notebook about that thing."

He didn't have a response to this that wasn't itself an example of the thing, so he put the binder away.

Brom, walking a half-step behind them both with the loose, ground-eating economy of someone who'd covered more ground on foot than either of them combined, said nothing for a while, and then: "First time leaving a place on purpose."

"You left Velmere."

"I left Velmere the way you leave a room that's on fire. This is different." He considered the road ahead, the hills doing their slow work of resolving into something with an actual shape. "This is walking toward something instead of away from something. Haven't done that before. Didn't know it felt different, but it does."

Cael let that settle. It was, he thought, an accurate description of what he was doing too, though it had taken Brom saying it plainly for him to recognize the shape of it in himself. He wrote it in the notebook before he could stop himself, which rather proved Lira's point, and did not mention that he'd done it.

"What do you think it'll be like," Lira asked, later, when the road had leveled out enough to walk three abreast instead of single file, "an actual academy. A real one, even if it's a small one."

"Structured," Cael said. "Predictable, in the ways that matter. Probably less predictable in the ways I haven't thought of yet."

"That's not an answer, that's a hedge."

"It's an honest hedge. I don't have enough data to answer the actual question." He considered it further, because she'd asked in the tone that meant she wanted the real attempt, not the deflection. "I think it'll be the first place that has rules about me instead of just reactions to me. I don't know yet whether that's better."

"It's better," Brom said, without hesitation, from behind them. "Reactions change every time you walk into a new room. Rules, you can learn. You're good at learning rules."

Cael didn't have an answer to that either, not right away, so he let it sit with the rest of the day's small, accumulating certainties, and kept walking.

The last stretch of the climb ran through terraced pasture, sheep-cropped and stitched with drystone, and the road's verges went from weeds to something that had been cut back within the month — the first evidence of maintenance they'd seen since the second morning, and evidence, therefore, of someone whose job it was to maintain things. A hill town resolved out of the slope above them piece by piece: slate roofs, smoke from midday cooking fires, a mill wheel turning somewhere out of sight with a slow wooden complaint they could hear before they could place it. The air had changed too, thinner and cleaner, carrying wet stone and cut grass and, faintly, bread.

"Town first, or academy first?" Lira asked, when the road forked — one branch bending toward the huddle of roofs, the other continuing up.

"Academy," Cael and Brom said together, and Lira laughed, short and real, and took the upper branch without breaking stride.

---

Greyvane appeared the way minor institutions announced themselves, which was to say: not with the confident scale of somewhere that expected to be found impressive, but with the plain fact of being exactly as large as it needed to be and no larger. The wall came first — old stone, waystation-era by the look of the coursing, weathered to a grey that took centuries rather than decades. The gate was newer, added at some point after the wall had stopped needing to keep anyone out and started needing to log who came in.

Cael catalogued it, because cataloguing was easier than sitting with what he was actually feeling, an irony he was aware of and unable to do much about. The wall: old waystation stone, load-bearing, unornamented. The gate: two generations newer at most, iron-strapped oak, a single clerk's booth built into the near side of it rather than a full guardhouse, which told him something about how much traffic this place actually saw. Beyond the gate, what he could see of the grounds: a training yard, walled separately, smaller than the Ironyard by a comfortable margin; a residence wing with the cramped, irregular window-spacing of a building retrofitted for dormitory use rather than built for it; a lecture wing further back, newer construction, the only part of the complex that looked like anyone had built it with Greyvane itself in mind rather than inheriting it from whatever the waystation had needed.

Smaller than the word *academy* had promised him, in the three days he'd had to build an idea of the place out of nothing but Quenna's account of it. More real for the smallness. He'd learned enough about the gap between what things were called and what they were to find the honesty of a small place calling itself accurately more reassuring than a large place's marketing might have been.

The light was doing the thing afternoon light did in hill country, coming in low and sideways and catching the wall's old stone at an angle that made the whole surface look faintly gold, and Cael stood there a moment longer than the walk required just watching it, because it was the kind of thing he'd have missed entirely a year and a half ago, and hadn't missed since Ardenmere taught him to notice what a place looked like before he decided what it meant. Somewhere beyond the wall a bell rang once, slow and unbothered, marking some hour of the academy's day that had nothing to do with their arrival, and the plain fact of an institution that had gone on being itself, on its own schedule, for sixty years before they'd ever heard its name, did something to settle the last of the road's nervous energy out of his shoulders.

The gate clerk had a list.

This, more than the wall or the gate or the modest scale of the grounds, was the thing that actually landed. The clerk — a woman perhaps forty, ink-stained at the first two fingers of her right hand in the manner of someone who spent her days transcribing rather than composing — ran a finger down a page, found what she was looking for, and said, without ceremony: "Three of you. Cael, unclassified observer track. Lira, re-certification candidate. Brom, standard enrollment." She looked up. "You're expected. Go on through — intake's the first door past the yard, someone will meet you."

Cael stood there a half-second longer than the moment required.

It was, he understood, a small thing. An administrative fact, ordinary logistics of the sort that made any institution function, no different in principle from the certification appointment the city of Denvash had scheduled for him a year and a half before he'd ever asked for it. But there was a difference between a door opening because the system required it to open on schedule, and a door opening because someone in particular had looked at his name in advance and decided it belonged on the other side. He had spent most of his life being processed. This was the first time, he thought, walking through the gate behind Lira and Brom with a lightness in his chest he didn't have a category for yet, that he'd arrived somewhere and found his name already waiting for him rather than something he had to prove into existence on-site.

He wrote it down, later, in different words. For now he just noticed it, and let it sit there, unfiled — which, for Cael, was itself an event worth noting.

---

Intake ran three lines, which made the asymmetry visible before anyone said a word about it.

The intake building itself was small and old — waystation stock, like the wall, with the low ceilings and thick-set doorframes of construction that predated anyone's current notion of how much headroom a person needed. Three desks, three clerks, a bench along the far wall where a handful of other arrivals sat waiting their own turn, and a window at the back that let in enough afternoon light to make the whole room feel less like a bureaucratic chamber and more like someone's well-organized study. Cael mapped the room out of habit while they waited to be called forward, and found himself, not for the first time, grateful that habit gave him something to do with his hands and his attention while the actual waiting happened underneath it.

Brom's line moved fastest. Application, record, stamp — the clerk handling his paperwork glanced at his Copper-tier certification, his clean transfer record from two seasons of documented circuit work, and processed him with the brisk efficiency of someone handling the most straightforward case she'd see all week. Eleven minutes, start to finish. Brom accepted his enrollment tag — a small stamped disc that would get him through most doors on the grounds without a second look — turned it once in his fingers, and allowed himself an expression that on anyone else might have been called smug, and on Brom registered as something closer to quiet astonishment that a process involving him could be this uncomplicated.

"That's it?" he said.

"That's it," the clerk said. "Orientation's at the fourth bell. Residence wing, east hall, your name's on a door."

Brom looked at the tag again, and then at Cael, with the look of a person who had spent two years being the complicated case in every room he'd entered and had genuinely not anticipated what it would feel like to be the simple one. "I don't know what to do with this," he said, meaning the tag, and possibly meaning something larger than the tag.

"Put it in your pocket," Lira said, from the next line over, not quite masking the strain in her own voice. "That's generally what people do with the uncomplicated ones."

Brom put it in his pocket, and then, a moment later, took it back out and looked at it once more, as though checking it hadn't quietly turned into something more complicated while he wasn't watching. It hadn't. He put it away again, properly this time, and went to go find the residence wing on his own, because there was, for once, nothing left in his day that required anyone's supervision.

Lira's line took the better part of an hour.

The re-certification provision, it turned out, required more than a stamp. It required her prior record — her Fenmark file, which had evidently traveled ahead of her by whatever channel academies used to communicate about students they'd expelled — cross-referenced against the provision's exact language, verified, and then, at the end of it, a single sheet placed in front of her that she had to sign. The clerk handling it was careful with her in a practiced, impersonal way, reading each clause aloud before asking her to confirm she understood it, and Cael watched Lira absorb the procedure standing perfectly still, arms folded, giving the desk nothing — the stance he'd seen her take a hundred times across a sparring circle from an opponent she hadn't finished measuring. The bench along the wall emptied and refilled twice while her file made its slow passage from one set of hands to another. Cael, standing close enough to see without meaning to intrude, caught the heading: *Acknowledgment of Prior Standing.* Beneath it, in the flat administrative language institutions used for things that mattered enormously to the people signing them and not at all to the people who'd drafted the form: a formal acknowledgment that her enrollment at Fenmark Academy had been terminated, that the termination stood as recorded, and that her candidacy here was contingent on accepting that record as accurate rather than disputed.

Lira read it twice. Her jaw did most of the talking during the second read — a muscle working at the hinge of it, the taut control of someone holding a much larger reaction down to a size that fit the room.

She'd spent two years refusing to say the word *expelled* about herself without immediately following it with an explanation of why the assessment had been wrong. She signed the sheet without a word, and the silence afterward said more than the explanation would have.

"That's the whole of it?" Cael asked, once she'd stepped back from the desk.

"That's the whole of it." She was looking somewhere past him, at nothing in particular. "Turns out being angry about a thing for two years doesn't actually change what you have to sign to move past it. Who knew."

"You could have told them the assessment was wrong."

"I could have. There's a box on the form for a formal dispute filing, if I want to pursue one later, separately, through channels that have nothing to do with getting enrolled here today." She exhaled, and something in her shoulders came down half an inch. "I decided I wanted to be enrolled here today more than I wanted to be right on paper about something that happened two years ago. That's new, for me. I'm choosing to think it's growth."

"It might be growth."

"Don't sound so surprised."

Cael did not, in fact, sound surprised, but he understood the comment was doing a different kind of work than a literal correction would address, so he let it stand.

She stood there a moment longer, the signed sheet already handed back and filed away behind the desk, out of her hands now in every sense that mattered. "Two years," she said, mostly to herself. "I thought I'd feel different, signing it. I thought it would feel like losing again."

"Does it?"

"No." She sounded almost surprised by her own answer. "It feels like putting something down I've been carrying since before I met you. Heavier than I let myself notice, until it wasn't there anymore." She shook her head once, like she was shaking off the thought before it could turn into something she'd have to examine standing in a hallway. "Go find out what they've decided you are. I'll be on the wall."

---

Cael's line took the rest of the day.

There was no queue for it, in the end, because there was no established process it fit cleanly into — which was, he understood by the second hour, the entire point of the category he'd been enrolled under. Quenna met him at the intake desk herself rather than delegating him to a clerk, and walked him through it personally, which he registered as either a courtesy or a liability depending on how he chose to read her attention.

The afternoon went in stages. Documents arrived at the table in twos and threes, each one requiring some verification the previous one hadn't anticipated; twice Quenna left him alone with a cup of tea going cold while she went to argue quietly with someone in a back room about which form a person with no formable classification was supposed to generate. The window's light moved from the back wall to the floor and started to thin. At some point the other clerks finished their days and left, nodding to Quenna on the way out with expressions Cael read as *better you than me*, and the intake room settled into the hush of a building doing overtime for exactly one case. He didn't mind the waiting, once he understood what it was: not obstruction, but a system straining, in good faith, to describe him. He'd been described in bad faith before. The difference was worth a long afternoon.

The enrollment document, when it finally reached the table in front of him, classified him as an *unclassified observer.*

He read the category line three times. It was a designation, the accompanying notes explained in the same flat administrative register that had governed Lira's acknowledgment sheet, reserved for prospective practitioners who had not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment — children, typically, whose families had petitioned for early academic exposure ahead of their certification appointment. It granted training-floor access, lecture attendance, and academic standing, without conferring any Path classification, tier ranking, or Arbiter-issued record. It was, on its face, a category built for someone who did not yet exist as a practitioner in any way the Compact's registry recognized.

Cael, fifteen years old, Iron-equivalent by the honest ledger of a year and a half of circuit work, four fragment entries filed in the binder currently sitting in his pack, read the words *has not yet undergone formal Kindling assessment* and did not say anything for a long moment.

"I underwent it," he said finally. "A year and a half ago. There's a record."

"There's a record with a classification in it that the Compact's own schedule doesn't define," Quenna said, evenly, the same even precision she'd brought to every conversation he'd had with her since the night she'd first sat down across from him at the Ironyard. "The provision's language covers 'any practitioner without a standard Arbiter-issued classification.' Yours isn't standard. It isn't, strictly, anything the schedule accounts for at all. That's not a loophole I invented, Cael. It's a gap that was already there. I'm walking you through the door it happens to open."

"It's still a lie. On the form. In writing."

"It's an accurate description of a category the Compact never built for someone like you, filled out using the closest available language, because the alternative was leaving you with no category at all." She held his gaze without softening it. "The provision's language is what protects you. What it says is defensible — the words on this page will hold up to scrutiny exactly as written. The provision's *intent*, though, is not the same thing as its language, and that's the part you'll be asked about, someday, by someone who's read this form more carefully than the clerk who processed Brom's did."

"Someone will look closely."

"Yes." No hesitation in it, no attempt to make the answer smaller than it was. "Our job, between now and then, is to be established fact by the time they do. A student with a monthly assessment record. A student whose demonstration performance speaks for itself, publicly, on a schedule anyone can verify. The longer you're simply *here*, doing well, on the record, the harder it becomes for anyone to argue you shouldn't be."

"And if someone argues it anyway."

"Then we'll have had months, at least, of established fact standing between you and whatever they're arguing." Quenna folded her hands on the table, an economical gesture that somehow closed the conversation without ending it. "I've made bets with worse odds than this one, Cael. I generally don't make them without believing they'll pay."

Cael looked at the form again. The category above the signature line was, by any honest accounting, not what he was. He thought, briefly, about the certification appointment in Denvash, a year and a half and a lifetime ago now, the four lines about [SHATTERED] classification that had sat in his memory ever since with the stubborn weight of a thing he'd never managed to write down properly. Four instances, in the whole recorded history of the registry. He was about to become, on paper, something the registry had never once had to account for at all — not a fifth instance of a known problem, but a category error the system's own language couldn't hold. It was a strange kind of progress, becoming a problem too new to have a file yet. He wasn't certain it was better than the alternative. He was fairly certain it was the only door currently open.

He was going to sign it anyway, because Quenna's math was better than his fear, and because he had already walked three days toward this door with two people who'd walked it with him rather than around him, and turning back now would have cost more than the lie on the page was asking him to carry.

He signed it. His handwriting was careful — the same hand he used for the fragment log, the hand that had learned somewhere along the way that permanence deserved precision even when precision couldn't fix what was underneath it.

The ink dried. Nobody at the table said anything about the gap between the words and the truth, because everybody at the table already knew it was there, and naming it again wouldn't have made the form any more honest.

"Welcome to Greyvane," Quenna said, and there was something almost gentle in it, underneath the procedural flatness. "Orientation's at the fourth bell. I'd get settled before then, if I were you."

---

He found Lira and Brom outside the intake building, sitting on a low wall in the last of the afternoon light. The three of them had found walls like it to sit on in more towns than he could easily count anymore, and something in him eased at the sight of this one already occupied, two silhouettes with a gap between them exactly his width.

"Well?" Lira said.

"I'm an unclassified observer," Cael said. "Officially. In writing. With a signature."

Brom considered this with the quiet interest of someone turning over an unfamiliar mechanism to see how it worked. "That's not what you are."

"No."

"Does that bother you?"

Cael thought about it honestly, which was the only way he knew how to think about anything that mattered. "Less than I expected. Quenna's right that the words hold up on their own, whatever they're covering for. I think I've spent enough of the last year and a half being something the paperwork didn't have a name for that one more form saying the wrong thing about me doesn't move the number much." He paused. "I'll probably still write about it tonight."

"You'll definitely still write about it tonight," Lira said, not unkindly.

"For what it's worth," Brom said, "I don't think the form is the interesting part."

"What's the interesting part."

"That they built a door for you at all. Most places just build walls and let you figure out you're not getting through them." He said it the way he said most of the things that turned out, on reflection, to be the most useful thing anyone had said all day — plainly, without dressing it up, like he was reporting a fact rather than offering comfort. "A wrong-shaped door's still a door. You've walked through worse."

Lira nudged his shoulder with hers, not quite a laugh but adjacent to one. "Look at you. Philosophizing before dinner."

"Don't get used to it," Brom said, and didn't sound entirely unhappy about the possibility that she might have to anyway.

"What's yours say?" Lira asked Cael, after a moment. "The tag. Or do observers not get one?"

Cael produced it — a disc like Brom's, except stamped with a hollow circle where Brom's carried the academy's crest in full. "Provisional access marker. It opens the same doors his does. It just tells anyone who inspects it that the question of why is above their station."

"Huh." She held it up against the fading light, turned it, handed it back. "A door-key shaped like an asterisk. That's you all over."

They sat a while longer, watching the last of the light go gold and then grey over walls that had stood through more than sixty years of students walking uncertain first evenings of one shape or another. Somewhere past the training yard a bell began to sound — not the fourth bell yet, Cael judged, something earlier, a signal for a rhythm of the day he hadn't learned to read yet.

He got out the notebook anyway, before the bell finished ringing, and wrote it down plainly, without ornament, in the hand he saved for things he meant to keep.

*Enrolled. The word is doing a lot of work.*

He looked at the sentence for a moment. Then, underneath it, smaller: *So am I. That's the deal.*

He closed the notebook, and went to find out what a fourth bell sounded like.

===== books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md =====
# Chapter 14 — Nineteen

He managed twenty-one lying on his left side on a plank table with the sun on the ceiling, and told her from the beginning, because the beginning was the only place he had ever found to start it.

Fourteen. A certification office in Denvash, a sigil that lit and went dark for eleven seconds and came back with a word on it nobody living had seen. A year in an Unranked District with a notebook. A training floor, and a girl with a Wind Path who did not know she was teaching, and a sidestep his body made before his foot decided to, and a page in the log with a question mark on it. He gave her the first notice, and the format of it, and that he had never once received the other kind. He gave her the count — nine before this morning — and the sources, by Path and not by name, because the names were the circle's and she had not been in the circle; and then, because she was a healer and had had her hands on three of them, he gave her three names anyway. Brom's. Karis's. Lira's. He told her what the conditions were as he had them, and that the model said real and not fight, and that until an hour ago nobody had ever tested that sentence.

She did not interrupt. She stood with her hands flat on the table's edge either side of his shoulder, leaning on them, and looked at the wall where the sun had been, and listened the way she had read: without a verdict attached, going along it.

"On your table," he said, "the conditions came together. I felt them come together, and I felt it start, and I could have stopped it, and I didn't. I reached. On purpose. The notice came while your hands were still on me." He said the notice to her, line by line: the exact wording that had stood on the wall. "*Clinical.* It's never said that before. It's never said anything that wasn't a fight, or the next thing to one."

"Tide-adjacent," Oryn said. It was the first thing she had said in twenty-one minutes, and it was a word she was trying for weight.

"Yes," he said.

"Partial."

"They're all partial. They're all *adjacent*. I've never had the whole of anything." He kept his eyes on the ceiling; the stillness was easier with the eyes fixed. "There's a page. I wrote it last night, before, in the loft, so that I'd have to pay what's on it and not what I felt like paying after. It's in the satchel. It's yours to read. It says what I knew and what I didn't and why I didn't tell you before, and the last reason isn't a good one, and it's on the page with the good ones."

"Get it."

He got up off the table.

---

He went up the loft ladder and came down it with the leather book, and she had not moved from where she stood; she had turned the stool to face the window and sat down on it, and she took the book from him without looking at him and opened it to the pencil.

He stood, and did not know where to put his hands, and put them at his sides, which was where she kept hers.

She read it as she read Karis's turned notebook: once, the whole of it, and then the parts that had a count in them a second time. *Sustained. Stakes. Earnest.* Her lips moved on the conditions. They did not move on the price. She read *she can't consent to what she can't know about* and stopped there for the length of one of her breaths, and read on, and read *the real one is that I don't know what she'd say*, and read on past that as if it were a wound she had already seen the extent of. Then *I want it. Write that too.* Then the last line. Then she closed the book on the pencil and held it shut on her knee with both hands, as she had held the arm.

"So I couldn't read you because there was nothing in you shaped like a Path for the reading to find."

"I don't know. Maybe."

"I don't know either. I'm a healer, not a theorist." She was looking at the book's cover and not at him. "What I know is you've now got a piece of the thing I read with."

"Yes."

The pause was a healer's. He had watched her take it over Lira's leg on the mountain and over Brom's forearm at the stone: the count going to its end, and the finding not said until it had.

"Then use it on yourself first. Before anyone." She looked up. "I want to know what you find, and I want to be the one you tell."

He had been asked for a great many things by a great many people about what he was — a demonstration, a transcript, a baseline, a sentence with the hole in the right place — and he stood in a steading's back room and catalogued that nobody had ever attached a condition to it before. Not a price; the Compact priced it. A condition: a thing he would have to do first, and a person he would have to tell, both hers to set, and both right. He had a page on her knee that said what he had chosen for her, and she had read it, and this was her answer, and it was the only one in the log that had ever come back with terms.

"Yes," he said. "First. And you."

"Good." Oryn gave him the book. "Now lie down again. Not for me. You're the colour of the wall and you've been standing on that calf for a quarter of an hour, and I've just been told I'm a patient's source, and I'd like a minute to sit with the second thing before I go and eat."

He lay down. She sat with it. Neither of them said anything for a while, and then she got up and opened the door, and the steading's woman was on the other side of it with a bowl, because the woman had known how long the long one took.

---

It came on at dusk, and it came in a register the log had no words for, so he built them on the first night and spent two days finding out whether they held.

The cascade was the cascade. He had banked for it since Ostrand, and the discipline held as it had held eight times: the circle told on the loft ladder before dark, in four sentences; nothing scheduled; water and the last of the bread within reach of the grain he lay on; and Brom on the ladder's head with the lantern turned down to a thread, because it was his turn, and the rota had never once been discussed.

What arrived was not a pull. He had had appetites before — motion, contact, the air, fixity — and every one of them had wanted something from him, and a want could be named and scheduled round and, in the end, put in an idle state and told to be quiet. This did not want. It *listened*.

He lay on his back on the sacks in the dark and heard himself.

Not with the ears. With the thing the tenth fragment had brought in from her hands: the sense of current, hers, turned on his own. At the edge of every thought, faint, there was a flow — his own — going along whatever it went along, and he could feel it as a man in a still house at night feels the house breathe. It did not stop. It had never stopped. He had lived inside it his whole life without once perceiving it, and now it was there at the edge of everything, and the strangeness was not that it was loud. It was that it had always been there, and that he was standing in a room he had lived in for eighteen years with the lights suddenly on.

Then the others came up out of it, one at a time, as the night went on, and that was the part he had not been ready for.

Wind, first. Not the framework, not the thing he deployed — the *flow* of it, the current that had been ringing faint since the Ring's second morning, audible now as a thing that ran, and ran a particular way, quick, and near the surface, so that he could have said where in himself it lay as Oryn had said it from outside: *the water's too fast*. Pressure under it, slower. Iron, deep and dense and very quiet, running like water under a floor. He lay and listened to each of them come up out of the general flow and be itself, and by the small hours he had nine, each with a sound that was not a sound, told apart as he told the five people in this loft apart by their breathing.

He said so to Brom, once, because Brom had asked him to say when it was bad, and this was not bad and he wanted that entered too.

"It's not bad. It's *loud*. Not loud like Storm was loud. Loud like a room with people in it that I've been calling empty."

Brom considered that from the ladder's head. "How many people."

"Nine. Ten. I can't hear the tenth. It's the thing I'm hearing with."

"Mm." The lantern's thread did not move. "My father could hear the forge from the house. Every hammer. Thirty years. My mother said she'd never once heard it and he said that was because she'd never once listened." A pause. "He didn't mean it kindly. But it was true."

He entered it, and entered under it, in the flat hand the log kept for things it did not like, that he had no way of knowing what a thing heard for the first time was *supposed* to sound like, and that *loud* was the wrong word and the only one he had.

---

The second day was the worst of it and it was not, by any measure the log had ever kept, bad.

The listening did not stop with the light. He went down the ladder and the currents came with him, and he stood in Thornwater's yard in the cold with the well in front of him and heard nine flows go about their business inside him while a woman drew water, and found that the difficulty was not pain. It was *attention*. Every framework he owned had an idle state, and the idle state had been taught, over four years, by a hand that could not perceive what it was putting to sleep — like tuning a string in a room where you could not hear it, by the feel of the peg. Now he could hear the string, and a hand that can hear every string in a room wants to go to each one, and the going was the cost.

Storm resettled, as it always did, being the heaviest thing he owned. He felt it go mid-morning, standing in the yard — the whole architecture shifting a hair round the new presence — and for the first time in his life he *heard* the settling happen, from inside, as a change in a flow he could follow, and stood with a hand on the well's rim until it had closed, which it did, on its own, inside a quarter of an hour. He wrote *correctly*, and then that he still could not have said what incorrect would sound like, and that at least now it would be a sound.

The Wind economy went with the resettling, as it had gone above Ostrand, and the yard's ground arrived a half-beat late under a foot that had been counting on it, and he stood still until it came back.

Oryn watched him do that from the house door.

She had done the steading's hurts that morning at the rate she had — a man's split thumb, a child with a cough she said was nothing and treated as if it were something, the woman's own knee, unmentioned — and she came out into the yard with her hands drying on the coat and stood a stride off and looked at him as at a thing with a rule in it she had not been told.

"Where is it loudest."

Nobody had ever asked him that. Karis asked *what* and *how many* and *at what interval*. Lira asked *is it bad*. This was a healer's question, aimed at a body, and he answered it as one.

"Here." The breastbone, where her front hand had been. "Under where you were. It's not louder there. It's *nearer*. Everything's nearer there."

"And you're standing with your hand on a well because."

"Because the floor was late."

She looked at the well, and at the foot he had put his weight on, and at his face, and nodded once, as at a finding that matched the last one.

"Does it hurt in the right places?"

"It doesn't hurt."

"That's not what I asked."

"It's in the right places," Cael said. "It's just that I didn't know they were places."

"And when it turns outward, you ask. Every time. I ask the dying."

"I ask first. Every time."

Oryn took that back into the house with her without writing anything down, and he entered it, because one of them had to.

---

Karis documented all of it, because that was her seat, and she did it on the afternoon of the third day in the loft, with the three boxes open round her and her voice back.

It had come back as Oryn had said: in pieces, hoarse, over days; and she had used it for one sentence in the loft the night before the reading and not again until the second morning, when she had said *charcoal* to Brom in a voice like a dry hinge and been so plainly pleased with it that Brom had gone and found her some. By the third day it was a low, torn, usable thing, and she used it, and the notebook lay open beside her for the words that would not come out whole.

"Tenth." She had the sheet on the middle box's lid, turned to face him: the columns, the curve she had been drawing since a training hall in Greyvane, ten points on it now. "First non-combat. First clinical. First one taken lying down." That last was not on the sheet, and she did not write it. "Source-grade Iron. The arithmetic says two days to an idle state and it's been two and a half, and the half's the new register — you've never had one that listens, and there's no comparison class for what it costs to learn to stop hearing something. So. Holding. The curve holds." She put her finger on the tenth point. "For now."

"You always enter that."

"I enter it because the model has no ceiling and a model with no ceiling is a model. And I'm entering something else this time, at the same weight, and I want you to hear it in my voice and not read it in my hand, because it's the sort of thing that reads as a complaint and isn't." She turned the sheet a little, so that the light off the loft's window lay across the curve. "Ten. Ten sources, ten Paths, and the tenth one does nothing in a fight, and the tenth one is the first one that has ever let anybody — you — look at the thing I've been charting from outside for four years. I have ten points on a curve and no instrument. You have an instrument now. It's not mine. It never will be. I'm entering that I know that."

"Karis," he said.

"I'm not asking you to use it on me." She said it before he could say anything, and her voice went on the last word and came back. "I'm telling you I've noticed that you could. That's all. It goes in the record as an observation about the researcher and not about the subject, and then I'm going to close the box."

She closed the box.

---

The birthday fell on the third day, and Lira instigated it, one-armed, against the last of Thornwater's autumn — and both were true at once, because both were.

She did it at the house door at midday, to the steading's woman, in the voice she used for a distance: "It's his birthday. Nineteen. We've got three days' marks and a healer and a mule, and I want your common room and whatever you'll sell us that isn't bread." The woman looked at the sling's knot, said "Well," which was a ledger line, and named a price, and Lira paid it out of the pouch one-handed without putting the pouch down, which she had been practising for a week and had not said so.

Brom acquired the food. He did it without any order ever being heard placed: he spoke to the woman's husband in the yard for a while about something that was not food, and came back an hour later with a boy carrying one end of a board, and on the board there were three kinds — a kid roasted whole from the hold's own pen, the first meat in a fortnight that had not been cut into sixths; apples baked soft with the last of a comb of honey in their middles; and a cheese, whole, in a cloth, from a hold that had three kinds of everything because it had a wall and a well and a woman who counted. He set the board down with the mended hand doing its share, looked at it, said, "Three," and sat.

The common room. He catalogued it, because he loved it, and that was how: a long room under the loft with the second chimney in it, the fire lit for them and not for the hold; the plank table; a bench either side; a window onto the yard with the light going yellow in it; a door that shut. Six places. Lira at the fire's end with the arm across her and the foot up on the bench, refusing to explain it. Brom beside the board. Karis with the notebook shut for once, on the bench, her throat gone from green to the yellow of the apples. Seln at the door end, where the two doors were, sitting like a man who had sat at tables all his life, eating what he was given. And Oryn.

Oryn sat at the table the way a woman sits who has sat at a great many tables and stayed at none of them. She had the seat that faced the yard, and she ate — properly, for the first time since the bend, as a person eats who has been spending and has stopped — and she watched the five of them be a unit, with the same look she had given the stone at the line: a thing with a rule in it. She said perhaps nine things all evening. Four of them were about food.

He was eighteen at the start of the meal and nineteen by its middle, and the middle was where he wrote it, at the table, in his own hand, with the pencil out of the spine and the book open on the board's edge between the cheese and the apples, per the custom now four birthdays old and the first kept without an institution's wall round it:

*Hundred and second day past the Line. Nineteen. Written at Thornwater, in the steading's common room, autumn, the fire lit for us. Witnesses: four of my own, and a healer who's a stop.*

"A stop," Oryn said, reading it upside down, as she had read the map. "Good. Write it down before it changes."

The carter came at the third apple.

The steading's boy put his head in at the door and said a word to Seln, who was nearest, and Seln went out and came back with a slip of thin paper folded to a palm's width, and put it on the board in front of Cael without a word. Pike's hand: the upright letters that priced the void roads, on the ledger's own stock, the copy the board-keeper sent east to a crew when a line went in, because the book was for him and the saying was for them.

*Recovery, Fallow road, broker's posting. Taken. Witnessed. Completed: incomplete — instruments not recovered — crew returned. Six went in. Six came out. No price paid.*

He read it twice, the second time for the count, because Pike's counts were the whole of Pike, and it said six. Then he set it beside a note he had read in a records hall on a bluff, in a different hand, that had closed a file with the words *the instruments will not see its like*, and he laid the two of them side by side in the place he kept such things: one closed complete, and just, by a man walking his own paper across a counter; one closed incomplete, and true, by a one-armed man who priced a crew by what came back, and had written on the copy the only line that mattered to him: that the number going in and the number coming out were the same.

He put it in the satchel against the leather book, flat, where the others were. There was nothing else in the carter's bag with his name on it. The ferry was a month and had been late, and the chain from a shop in Denvash to a steading two days east of a town on no map was a long one. He did not look at the door twice. Lira, who had watched him not look, cut the cheese one-handed against her knee, and gave him the first piece, which was the size of two.

"Nineteen," Brom said, to the board, as a weight.

"Nineteen," said Karis, in the dry-hinge voice, and it went into the notebook after all, because some things did.

Seln, at the door end, looked at the satchel, and said, to nobody, in the voice for an inventory: "Six went in. Six came out. He's counted her." He went back to his plate. "So has the broker."

Nobody answered that, and Lira refilled his cup, which was an answer.

It ran until the fire was down to a working red. Oryn told them, when asked, the seven holds in order, and what each owed her and what she owed each, and it was the longest she had spoken since the bend, and she did it without once saying she was going back to it, because that did not need saying. Lira kept the taped foot on the bench and dared anyone to remark on the arm. Karis and Brom argued for a quarter of an hour, in one hoarse voice and one deep one, about whether an apple baked with honey in it was a thing the hold had *made* or a thing it had *had*, and Karis lost on the merits and said so. Seln left first, with a nod that took in the whole table and stayed on the healer a breath longer than the rest, and at the door he did not turn round.

He caught himself, somewhere past the middle of it, running the room as an instrument — six places, one board, two doors, a fire, the light in the window gone from yellow to nothing — and put the instrument down. Not the new one. He did not have a hand on that yet. The old one; the count. He sat in a common room in a walled steading two days east of Lowmarch, with five people who knew both his ledgers, on a birthday nobody with a seal knew the date of, and for the better part of an hour did nothing with any of it, which was the second time this year and the first time on ground no registry had drawn.

---

He did the thing she had asked him to do that night, alone, in the loft, with the five of them asleep round him and the lantern out, because she had said *first* and *before anyone* and he had said *yes*.

He had not tried it in three days; the currents had come up out of the general flow on their own, and he had let them and learned their sounds, and had not once turned the new thing on himself on purpose, because on purpose was the reading, and the reading was hers, and she had set a condition on it. He met it now. He lay on his back on the sacks with one hand flat on his sternum and the other under his back as far as it would go, which was not far, and did what she had done: went in, and went along.

Ten. He counted them because he counted things, and it came out ten: the nine he had learned by ear in three nights, and the tenth, which he could feel now that he was using it, as a man feels his own eye when he shuts the other. He could not find their banks. That was the first finding and it was hers already, from outside; from inside it was the same. He went along Wind and could not say where Wind stopped and Pressure began; he went along Iron and found it running under everything, and could not find the floor it ran under. Ten flows, and no edge to any of them, and the not-finding was a fact about the reading and not about the thing read, and he made himself write that down before he wrote anything else, so that it would be on the page above whatever came next.

Then he went to the place session nine had logged.

He knew where it was. He had known for five years, like a man knowing where a step on a stair is uneven: a place in himself that Brom's Iron read had caught the edge of once on a floor in Ardenmere, with no Tide practitioner in the city, at no stakes, and had never caught again. He had been round it a thousand times since without seeing it, as he had been round every current in him for eighteen years without hearing one. He went to it now with the new thing, slowly, the way she had gone along the ninth, and found it.

It was there.

He lay very still with his hands on himself and catalogued it as sensation, because he had promised her the finding and had promised the log no verb. It was not a current. That was the first thing and the plainest: everything else in him *went*, and this did not. It was not the tenth; the tenth was what he was feeling it with, and a hand is not the thing it touches. It was not any of the nine; he had their sounds now, and this had none. It was a place. Near the middle — under where her front hand had been, where everything was *nearer* — and the ten flows went round it. Not through. Round, the way the currents in a river go round a thing on the bed that they have gone round for so long that the going-round is the river's shape and not the thing's.

He did not name it. He did not have a name that was not a guess, and a guess was what the registry had done to him at fourteen.

He knew three things about it that were not guesses, and he counted them. It had been there before Oryn; session nine was five years old and she was two months. It had been there before Brom's read caught it, because a read catches what is already there. And it was not the Quiet — the Quiet was outside him, a line on the ground that stopped Paths, and this was inside, and the fragments went round it.

He took his hands off himself, and felt, from his own side, the shape of the reading going, as he had felt hers go, and then that was gone too, and he was a man on a grain sack in a loft with five people breathing round him and a healer's condition met.

---

He wrote the inventory by the last of the lantern's oil, which the woman had refilled without being asked, in the plain hand.

*Hundred and second day, late. Thornwater, the loft. A hundred and two in hand; six to the hold for the room and the kid; nothing earned. Pike's copy in the satchel: six went in, six came out, no price paid. Nineteen.*

*Inventory. Ten confirmed. Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected; the seal holds out here as it held inside; used once past the Line, on a floor, in front of the circle and nobody else. Storm-adjacent — Daeva; resettled once this week round the new neighbour, on the second morning, in the yard, and I heard it do it. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; it wanted the stake and it can want. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only non-hostile in the log until three days ago. Tide-adjacent — Oryn. Partial. Iron. Self and contact. Engagement: clinical. Does nothing in a fight, and is the first thing I've ever owned that lets me hear the other nine. Cost: three days of listening; the idle state learned by the third night, and it isn't quiet, it's just that I've stopped putting my hand to every string. Deployment notes current on all ten.*

*Karis says the curve holds, for now, and that I have an instrument and she doesn't. Both entered.*

*The condition, in parts. Self first: done. Ask first: said in the yard, untested. Tell her: owed. What I found goes below, as what it is and not what it means.*

*The anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Turned over — for the first time in five years, with a Tide-adjacent fragment in hand to turn it over with. The three words are retired. They were the right three for a thing I couldn't see and could only wait on; I've had them at the foot of every inventory since Ardenmere, and they've done their work, and I'm taking them out tonight for a reason, so here's the reason.*

*Still open. Still real. Now I can see it. That's worse. The tenth fragment lets me feel my own currents, and there's one in the place session nine logged, and it isn't the tenth, and it isn't any of the nine. It was there before Oryn. It was there before Brom's read caught it. I don't know what it is. I know where it is now. Patience is over; observation begins.*

*Daeva's rematch, Reydan's answer — both ride east. Nineteen. Ten fragments. Hesk's package is on a ferry somewhere. The last one said mind the shape. I'm starting to.*

===== books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md =====
# Chapter 24 — Alignment

The snow came in the night before the ferry's last run, and he catalogued Lowmarch under it from the landing because he had catalogued it from a rise in four weathers and wanted the fifth to be the last one honest.

The river running black between white banks, with skin ice along the shallows that the ferry's bow would break on the way out and would not break on the way back, because there would not be a way back until the thaw. The landing with its bales gone, all of them, and the broker's mark under a hand of snow on the one crate that was left. The meadow white and empty, the tent-rings under it. Smoke from every roof. The board's room at the lower inn with both doors shut against the cold and the road door's hand's-width gone, so that a man who wanted the smoke out would have to want the cold in, and Pike, who kept the room, had chosen the smoke. The ferryman at his ropes with a boy, and the tarred sack on the deck with the Line's lead on it, waiting for the last of what was going west before the ice shut the door.

Five of them, on the hundred and twenty-ninth day past the Line, at the landing, in the snow, with a mule and three boxes and a survey kit and a girl with her arm across her chest, because first snow was first snow and a healer had said she would say when, and had not yet said.

He catalogued the five of them at the landing as he had catalogued them at every halt of the year, because it was the last halt and he wanted the count. Lira, one-armed, the sling gone grey with five weeks of road, the taped foot put down flat in the snow, Oryn's crossbow across her back on its strap; she had walked six days from the fourth site on that foot and had not once said the word *ankle*, and would not until a healer said the other word first. Brom, with the kit on one shoulder and the middle box on the same one, and the other shoulder carried in the same guarded set it had kept since a hall — still, and slightly forward — though the healer of no Path at Teague's fire had said it wanted binding and not hands, and it had been bound, and had healed under the binding as a shoulder heals when it is left alone by a man who cannot leave things alone. Karis with the three copies rolled under her arm in oilcloth against the snow, the ledger inside her coat where Seln had let her carry it for the last two days, and the straightedge in her hand, because she had not once put it back in the box since the marsh. Seln with the case on his back and his eyes on the landing door, counting who came out of it. And himself, with nothing in his hands, and ten things held to nothing, and the ribs quiet again since the marsh, and a still place at the middle of them that he had read three times and had not touched since, because a healer had said *stop* and he had stopped.

---

Pike read the season's line before he entered it, because the procedure required it, and the procedure was the only thing at Lowmarch that had never once bent for the weather.

He read it at the plank, to the book, in the voice he used for a price, with the room full because the room was where the warm was and the five of them at the plank because it was theirs, and he read the whole of it at the size a line was read, no larger.

"*Crew of six. Ford cleared, traverse mapped, Ring entered, Stair entered and instruments recovered, Hall entered. No losses. Returned every time.*"

The pen stopped. "That's said."

"That's said," Cael said.

He read the page upside down across the plank, because he read everything upside down and had never once stopped: nine completed lines under the five names, the last three of them the traverse, the Ring, and the Stair, in Pike's hand, each with its outcome and its price, and the Stair's price column saying *three hundred; taken; not paid*, in the small hand Pike used for a thing that was true and was not going to change. Pike put two fingers flat on the ledger and turned it, with one push, so that it faced across the plank, which he had done twice in a season, and Cael read it the right way up, and it said what it had said.

Pike did not write a number.

He had the wall sheet on the plank beside the book — the thirteen crews in the one-armed hand, top to bottom, Teague's four at the top where they had been for three years, and the five names eleventh, where he had put them after the Ring — and he had the pen over it, and he did not put it down, and the room, which had been not-looking at the five of them since the Stair in the way a room does not look at a thing it has decided about, stopped pretending it was not looking at the pen.

Pike said, to the sheet: "Teague says you need a column I don't have. I've kept this ledger thirty years. First time it's been wrong about a crew by being too small."

He put the pen down. He did not enter a line number. The five names stayed where they were, eleventh, on a sheet that had thirteen crews on it and a column for outcome and no column for whatever it was that walked into three floors and came out, and Pike pinned the sheet where it went, by the road door, unchanged, and stood back from it as he stood back from a price he had just said, and that was the saying, and the saying was for the room, and the room had heard it.

Cael filed it beside a note in a records hall on a bluff that had closed a file with *the instruments will not see its like*, and beside a captain on a road at dark saying *no column*, and found that the shelf had three ledger-keepers on it now, and that the third had just done the only honest thing a ledger can do with a thing it cannot hold, which is to say so aloud and leave the line blank.

The room had heard it, and the room did what a room does with a thing it has decided about, which is nothing, aloud. The hide-woman at the middle table with her ledger shut on her thumb, who had looked at the five of them in the autumn as at a thing that would be gone by the snow, looked at them now with the snow on the window and did not open her ledger. The crew of three by the road door, whose canvas had gone from new to grey to patched, had stopped eating. Two carters at the fire, one of whom had walked the Thornwater road behind them in the first week, looked at the wall sheet and at the five names eleventh on it and at the one-armed man standing back from it, and one of them said, to the other, at the size a carter says a thing about a road, "Eleventh," and the other said, "Aye," and neither of them meant the number.

---

Teague wintered at Lowmarch, as Teague's crew wintered every year, at the best table, with the jug, and he came to the plank while Pike was still standing back from the sheet, because a captain who has set a term does not wait for a better moment to say it.

He stopped a stride and a half off. He did not look at the sheet.

Teague said: "Spring. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes."

It was not an offer. It was a term, stated once, by a man who kept his own ledger and had put a line in it at a rim and had come three days along a bearing with a healer of no Path to bind a shoulder, and who now, at a plank in the snow, with the tall woman and the broad man and the young one at the table behind him not looking at the plank, as a crew does not look at its captain setting a term, told the crew at the bottom of a wall where he would be when the ice went out.

"Noted," Cael said.

Teague went back to his table, where his three sat with the jug and the fifth — the older woman with the cloth bag, who had bound a shoulder at a marsh and had walked back to Lowmarch with them and had not once said a word to any of the five since — and who looked up as her captain sat down, and at Brom's shoulder across the room, once, the way she had looked at it at the marsh, and went back to her cup, which was a healer's whole verdict and Brom took it as one.

Nobody at the plank said anything, because there was nothing to say that the word had not said, and Seln, at the door end, who had been watching the rival clock since a board's room in the autumn, looked at the man's back for the length of a breath and then at the snow through the landing door's crack, and Cael saw the look and did not have a column for it either, and let it stand.

---

The winter's contract went on the plank before the inventory went in the satchel, because that was the order the year kept: the board first, then the book.

Pike had one slip that had waited since the autumn — the axe-head hold's long fence, forty marks, HOLD, Teague, which Teague's crew had walked in the autumn and had not finished because the Stair had taken them south — and Pike said, to the book, "Fence. Forty. Teague says it's yours if you want it; he's got the winter road," and Brom, who priced fences, said, "Ours," and Pike wrote HOLD and the five names under it, and the winter had a job in it, which was the thing a winter needed. Karis took the inn's back room for the sheets, at a price the woman who kept the inn named and Karis paid without counting, which Cael had never once seen her do with money. Seln bought nothing, and sat at the door end, and watched the landing, and when the ferryman came in to say the sack was closing at noon, Seln looked at the sack and at his own coat where the ledger had been and was not, and said nothing, because he had given the ledger to Karis on the road and had not asked for it back and was not going to.

The inventory went into the satchel by the inn's fire that evening, by lamp, with the five of them round it and the sixth on a route, and it was the fullest the log had ever carried, and he wrote it as an inventory and not as anything else, because the ledger of the year wanted its forms kept.

Hesk's satchel. He had carried it out of a residence room on a bluff a year ago with the log in the pocket built for it, and it had been on every road since, waxed leather over a frame built for forty years of weather, the spare buckle still sewn flat on the strap's underside where it would not chafe, and the leather had gone dark along the strap where his shoulder took it and the brass had gone dull at every corner and not one seam had opened, because a man who had watched a strap fail on a road had built it so that it would not fail on anyone he liked. He set it on his knee by the fire, and opened the flap, and took the log out, and the four of them let him, because the inventory was a thing they had watched him write at the end of every year since Greyvane and had learned to sit still for.

*Hundred and twenty-ninth day. Lowmarch, the lower inn, by the fire. First snow. The ferry's last run tomorrow. Ninety-six in hand; six to the inn for the back room; nothing owed; nothing earned since the Ring, and three hundred entered as taken and never to be paid. Nineteen.*

*Ten confirmed fragments.*

*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*

*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.*

*Reydan will get his answer. Daeva will get her rematch. Both are going to need a map.*

He read it back once, and closed the book on the pencil, and put it in the satchel's pocket built for it, and closed the flap, which closed against nothing, because the snow was outside.

---

The ferry's last run took the mail west and brought the mail east, and the ferryman sorted it aloud at the plank by name, as he had sorted it four times since the autumn: the tarred sack opened on the plank, the Line's lead broken by Pike with the knife he kept for the pen, and the pieces taken out one at a time and the name on each said at the size a price was said, so that the room could hear who had been written to and who had not. Nine pieces. Six for carters who were wintering at holds and would have theirs carried out by the last carts. One for the inn. And two for the five of them, and one of the two was not a letter.

Hesk's package was the size of a hand.

He had known it by the wrapping before the ferryman said the name — the oiled cloth, the twine tied the way an instrument-maker ties twine, so that it comes off in one pull and can be tied again — and he took it to the fire and did not open it at the plank, because he had never once opened a thing of Hesk's at a plank. He opened it on his knee with the log beside it, because that was where things went when he had read them.

A straightedge. Brass, folding, three sections on two pins so that it lay in the hand the length of a palm and opened to the length of a forearm, and the edge true — he sighted along it against the fire and there was no light under the line — and calibrated, in the small struck marks he had known before he could read, along its whole length, in a hand that had cut the marks on every gauge in a shop in Denvash for forty years. Instrument-maker's work. Six weeks late.

The note was in the fold, in the upright letters:

*Nineteen. I'm told it's late; the ferry's fault, not mine. Your grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. This one's true to a hair. Mind the shape. — H.*

He read it twice. Then he looked across the inn's table at Karis's map, which was under her hands with three circles on it and a fourth and a line through all four, and at the brass straightedge from Ternhall lying along the line, and at the brass straightedge from Denvash in his own hand, and he did not say anything, and Lira, who had read the note aloud per the family's open post and had watched him look, did not say anything either, and Karis looked up from the map, once, at the thing in his hand, and went back to the map, which was, from Karis, a whole sentence.

He wrote west that night, by the fire, on the inn's paper, to a shop in Denvash, in the plain hand, short, because the ferry would carry a paragraph and would not carry a page:

*Nineteen, past the Line, and there's a roof. It's an inn. The shape you said to mind is a line, and I've got the tool for it now. — C.*

And to Vell, at Ardenmere, because her ledger held the first honest record of him that had ever been written and Pike's held the newest, and she would want to know the thing that a ledger-keeper wants to know about another one:

*Vell. There's a second ledger on the continent with my name in it, kept by a one-armed man in a town on no map, and this week he stopped numbering me. He said his book was wrong about us by being too small. You said once that somebody should have a record that doesn't answer to the Compact. There are two now. The session still stands. — C.*

He sealed both, and put them in the tarred sack himself, at the landing, in the snow, and watched the ferryman put the Line's lead on it.

---

The second piece for the five of them came by carter, not by ferry, the morning after, up the void road from the east with a hide-load and a mule, and the carter said the name at the plank and Pike said, "That one's here," and the carter gave it to Cael because Cael was the one who had come to the plank.

Thornwater's paper. A hand that had set a great many bones, square and plain, written to be read by people who did not read much, and it was four lines, and he read it at the plank, upside down first, because he read everything upside down, and then the right way up, and then aloud, to the four of them, because it was addressed to the five and the five kept an open post.

*Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. Spring: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*

Six, by letter.

He read it a fourth time to himself, because it was four lines and he wanted them: *Route's whole* — the seven holds walked round, the circuit closed, the thing she owned kept. *The knee held* — the old woman at Thornwater, up her ladder. *Nobody died of three weeks* — the cost she had laid flat on a bank at Millrace, paid, and found to be what she had said it might be and not what she had feared. *Spring: bring the map* — the same sentence as the fork, and the fork's sentence had meant the opposite of Oxhollow's, and this one meant the same as the fork's, and he had the whole of her in four lines and did not need a fifth. And *lie still when I say*, which was her terms of service, restated by post, so that the winter would not soften them.

He put it in the satchel, flat, against the leather book, where the others were, and Lira, one-armed, said, "She'll say when," meaning the arm, and meaning the spring, and Brom said, "Spring," meaning both, and that was the sixth chair filled, by post, at a plank, in the snow, because that was the only way she would ever have filled it, and everyone at the plank knew it, and nobody said so.

---

The map was on the inn's table, and it was the last thing.

Karis had laid it out at dusk without saying she was going to, the way she had laid the ring flat on Thornwater's table and the walls flat on the Stair's floor: the carter's sheet, with the four circles on it — the Ring, the Stair, the Hall, the fourth — and three of the centres marked to a stride in her small hand and the fourth as the carter had drawn it, near enough, and the kit's bearing drawn in pencil from the first two and running on, and the brass straightedge from Ternhall laid along the bearing, and now, beside it, the brass straightedge from Denvash, which Cael had opened and laid along the same line without being asked, and which touched the three centres without a hair of daylight and the fourth as near as the sheet allowed, exactly as the other did, because a thing that is true to a hair is true to a hair whichever hand cut the marks on it.

The five of them stood round it. Nobody had called them to. The lamp was on the table's corner and the fire was down to a working red and the snow was on the window, and the four circles lay on the sheet in a line, and the line went on past the fourth toward the map's edge and off it.

The sites were on an alignment. Not random.

Karis looked at it for a long time. She had the pencil in her hand and she did not use it, and she had the notebook under her other hand and she did not open it, and when she spoke it was the only sentence the evening permitted her, and she said it in the small voice, and once.

"Someone made this pattern."

Nobody answered. Nobody said who, or why, or how old. Lira looked at the line. Brom looked at the straightedges, both of them, brass on brass. Seln looked at the map's edge where the line went off it. Cael looked at the fourth circle, twice the size of the others, with a cordon round it that nobody had drawn on the sheet because nobody had needed to. The lamp's light lay along the brass and the brass laid its shadow along the line, a hair's width, the length of the map, and the five of them looked at the shadow for four seconds, by his count, and then Karis put her hand flat on the sheet, and the evening closed.

---

"I have ten things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no file I can see — and five people, one of them on a route, who've walked into the quiet three times and come out." He turned it over once and let it stand. "And a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one."

Lira, one-armed, feeding the fire: "Then we follow it."

Brom: "Spring."

Karis, writing: "Noted."

Down the fire, the man who used to file reports about him watched the snow on the ferry landing with evident professional approval and said nothing, which — from him — was home.

Nothing out here had read him. At three floors older than any registry, every Path in the party had stopped at a line and his had not; a fourth had been paced from outside and not crossed. Nobody alive — least of all him — knew why. Someone had made the pattern. That was as far as anyone could say.

