editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-48.md:1:# Chapter 48 — Declined to Be Interesting

--- suspicious asterisk adjacency ---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-48.md:11:It was slow work, slower than the standing had been, because there was no way to plant and arrive and ask without stopping, and stopping in the middle of a circling man was exactly the thing a fight would never let him do for long. So he learned to make the stop small. He learned to let the left foot come down as Brom's weight came round, and to let the knock go out in the instant between the foot touching and the next step beginning, a gap so short that he could not have measured it with a pulse. When he caught the gap, the answer came back nearly as clean as it had at rest: *inward*, *nothing*, *inward*, and once *going left*, as Brom threw the slow push at the wall instead of at him to see if Cael would notice. When he missed the gap, it was smear, or it was the stone floor answering instead of the man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-48.md:53:"Here. Halfway down." He read it out in his flat careful voice. "*In the spring of the second season the subject lost once, at the river halls, to an opponent of lower standing whom the writer had not expected to trouble him. The opponent did not attempt to solve the subject. For four exchanges he declined to be interesting: he gave ground, gave nothing new, offered the subject no approach to read and no plan to discard. The subject appeared unsettled by this to a degree the writer had not seen before. In the fifth the subject, pressing for something to answer, overcommitted, and was caught.*" Brom looked up. "And then: *The subject beat the same opponent in the rematch, three months later, in the second exchange.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-48.md:89:And under that, after a while, in ink: *I don't wait any more. I used to. Find out whether I still can.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-48.md:181:At the crate desk, very late, he took out the Log and turned to the two columns at the back. The question mark was still there in pencil beside *The right is only ever my feet*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-48.md:183:He rubbed it out, and then drew a line through the whole entry under *Shape*, a single line, so that it could still be read. He wrote it again at the foot of *Habit*, with the cost beside it the way every habit had a cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-47.md:63:So the second session was that: Brom standing a forearm off, gathering and sending, slowly at first and then less slowly, and Cael knocking at the moment he thought the gathering began, and saying what came back before the push arrived. *Inward*, *nothing*, *inward*, *smear*, *nothing*, *inward*, on and on. The inward answer was like nothing the knock had brought back before, neither *there* nor *going forward*. It was a kind of drawing tight, as though the whole man had become a fist in the making, and it came and went so fast at the edges that he could not have said whether he felt it begin or only felt it already begun. Twice he knocked too late and got the push instead, the weight going outward, already spent. Once he knocked too early and got only Brom, large and still and thinking about nothing, and had to say *nothing* while the gathering began a heartbeat after under his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-47.md:77:"Yes. But only when." He turned the hand over. "When you send, there's only one place it ever goes. At me, straight, because we're standing a forearm apart and you're aiming at my chest. So when the knock says *inward*, I know everything, because I already know where it's going. He won't be standing still in front of me. He'll be able to fire it anywhere." He looked at Brom. "The knock catches the pulling in. The pulling in is the same whichever way he means to let it go. The direction doesn't exist yet when I knock. It only exists when he fires."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-47.md:171:The heavyset man's wife began leaving a covered bowl on the range for him at night, without being asked and without saying so. He found it there after the evening hour with Lira, still warm, with a plate over it. On the second night the sister had written on the plate in chalk, *EAT, L SAYS*, and drawn a hen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-47.md:197:It was not that Brom moved quickly. He moved the way he always moved, as if every step had been weighed first, circling the alcove slowly with his weight low, so that Cael had to move with him to stay a forearm off. But a forearm off was no longer a place; it was a thing Cael had to keep making, every step, and every step he made unsettled the stillness the knock was built on. He planted his foot to knock, as Brom had taught him, and the knock went out, and half the time the answer came back as smear, or as nothing, or as a confused *there* that did not know whether it meant the man or the wall behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-47.md:213:*Nine in twenty, moving,* he wrote that night. *Of the eleven that didn't answer, six went out with my foot still coming down. The read isn't bad in motion. My knocking is. Plant, arrive, then ask.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-47.md:215:Under it, smaller, he wrote: *Six redirects done, six to go.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-49.md:55:Somebody on the second bench said *oh*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-49.md:63:He had planted his left foot at the end of a step, and arrived, and let the knock go out in the space before the next step began, the way he had been learning to do for six days with a man circling him. Ansel was a pace and a little more away, a distance at which the knock had always been poor. But Ansel was coming forward, closing the pace as Cael knocked, and the answer came back clean through the closing: *there, coming, heavy on the right*. Cael went left before the right hand had started, and the hook went by in the air, and he was out on Ansel's side with the open ribs in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-49.md:85:It was terrible. Every heartbeat of it his body was telling him to go and find out. Twice he caught his own weight beginning to go forward of its own accord, toward a gap he had seen in Ansel's guard, and stopped it, and felt the stopping in his whole frame like a cart dragged up short. The benches did not understand it. He heard somebody on the second bench say *what's he doing*, and somebody else say *nothing*, and the first say *well that's no good*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-49.md:93:Cael felt it go before it went. He knocked from a planted foot at a pace and the knock came back with a kind of urgency in it that it had never given him before: *there, coming, all of it, now*. Ansel came in with everything, the whole of the quick close work at once, left and right and left. It was too much and too far, because he had waited too long and wanted it too badly, and his weight went a hand past where it ought to have stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-49.md:95:Cael was not in front of it. He had gone left at the knock, on the hip, a read burst, front-left round the outside of the first hook, half a body. In the lock, the two *ands* of bright stillness at the end of it, he saw what he had known he would see. Ansel's whole frame had gone past him with nothing behind it, and the back of his ribs was open, and his feet were wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-49.md:113:Ansel came at full speed, as he had in the first, with nothing held back, and Cael met it with everything Lira had put into him in eight days. He dropped and rolled right under the first hook. He knocked from a planted foot at a pace and got *there, heavy left* and went right on his feet, a beat slow still but not a whole beat. He took a short hook on the forearm that numbed it to the elbow, and another on the shoulder he had spent nine redirects on, and felt the shoulder tell him so. Once he knocked and got smear, a confused rush of weight that did not know which way it was going. Because he had decided before the round that smear meant *don't go*, he did not go. He stood off and took a straight on the forearm instead of walking into the hook behind it, and was glad, after, of the bruise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-49.md:115:At the end, Ansel came in once more with the whole of his quick work, and Cael knocked and got it clean, *right, coming, all of it*. He dropped and rolled right, under it and over, and came up off the right foot into the open side with a short strike that he stopped against the cloth of Ansel's shirt over the ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-49.md:165:He wrote it before he iced the arm, sitting on the bench in the alcove with the Log on his knee and the forearm going stiff. *Ansel, with his leave. At his speed, a pace and closing. Six knocked, four answered, one smear (didn't go; right), one late. Stood off the whole of the second and gave him nothing. He ran out of waiting before I did. I didn't know I still could.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-49.md:209:He knocked on the next one. He planted his left foot at the end of a short step and let the knock go out toward her as she began. It came back cleaner than anything Brom had ever given him, *there, coming, all of her, left*, quick and small and alive, like a bird going off a sill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-49.md:215:He knocked on thirteen of the twenty she sent him that morning, and eleven of the thirteen answered, and he called every one of those aloud. The other seven he did not knock at all, because his feet had already gone, and a knock would have shut him in place to ask. He found that the drop and the knock would not share any better than the hip and the knock had, and wrote it in the Log at noon. *Knock, shut, then go: still true. The drop is a go. Don't knock while I'm already dropping. Choose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-44.md:17:He had the Log open at the back, at the two columns he had ruled on the night Bede beat him. The *Habit* column, for things he did and could train out at a price, had five lines in it now, and a cost against each. The *Shape* column, for the way the borrowed things had been built in him, had five lines and no costs, because shapes could not be bought out, and under each he had been trying for a fortnight to write how he would stand so that nobody was ever in the place it left open. Four of the five had something under them; the fifth did not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-44.md:29:"And you've put it under *Shape*." She tapped the column. "Your hip only goes left. That's how the Wind was made in you; it came out of my left hip and my barn master's before me, and you can't argue with it. Fine. But your feet aren't the Wind. Your feet are only feet." She looked up at him. "Nobody built them slow going right. You did, by never asking them. That's a habit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-44.md:57:Reydan noticed that. He noticed that nobody else in the room seemed to find it strange. He noticed that when she said the words she said before every bout, the two fighters both answered her aloud, and that she waited for the answer, and that the room went quiet while she waited. He did not catch every word. He caught *the rule in this room*, and he caught the quiet, and he put both away to look at later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-44.md:63:He did not watch the strikes. He had stopped watching strikes at about fourteen, when a master at the academy had stood behind him at a sparring floor and put two fingers on the back of his neck and turned his head, gently, from the blades to the feet. *The blade tells you what happened,* she had said. *The feet tell you what's about to. And between the exchanges, when nobody's looking, the feet tell you what the fighter's afraid of.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-44.md:109:Reydan had read it standing in the lamp-oil shop with the smell of the oil all round him. The story had said *no Path*, and the story had been told by fools. But a keeper's line was not a story. A keeper did not write *Iron-equivalent* in a book that other keepers read because a crowd had shouted. And *No Path designation* in a keeper's careful hand was not a boast or a legend. It was simply what was there, set down plainly, by somebody whose whole life was being right about such things. He had read it twice, and then gone home and packed one bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:11:The row had changed again in the four days since the betting man's split line. Two of the food stalls by the pump had begun to bake in larger batches, and the pie woman had a boy helping her who had not been there before. Outside the Ironyard's side door, when Cael passed it, two men he knew by sight from the eel market were standing with Dace in the cold, talking with their hands, and Dace was listening with his pencil against his chest and the face he wore when he was being asked for something he had never given anybody. Cael heard *inside the doors*, and *three hours on their feet*, and *a copper a bowl*, and slowed without meaning to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:13:Dace saw him over the men's shoulders and lifted the pencil an inch, which meant *wait*. The two men went off toward the eel market looking pleased with themselves, and Dace came over to him with the expression of a man who has just sold something he had sworn never to sell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:21:The paper-stall man asked him again whether he was feeling well, with less care this time, and Cael said *very*, and the man nodded and let the slate line stand where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:23:All of it was a long way off. He noticed it the way he noticed weather, as a thing that was happening round him, and carried the bread back down the hill, and ate it on the bench in the alcove with Lira's staff leaning on the wall beside him. He had knocked her thirteen times that morning, with her leave, and twelve had answered, and he had called every one aloud. She had said *yes* to each without stopping, and at the end she had said *twelve*, and nothing else, and gone up to sleep for an hour before her own day. That was what mattered this morning, and the pennant in his coat did not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:25:It had been the strangest of all the mornings, because with her leave given the alcove had become a different room. Every time she came at him he could feel her coming for an instant before she came, small and quick and alive at the end of his knock, and every time he called it aloud she said *yes* without breaking stride, so that the morning had gone by in a kind of conversation, his voice and her staff and the one word back. He had not been hit nearly as often as he had been all week, but hard enough, the times he was, to remember that a knock was not a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:43:*Inward*, and he was already turning, letting the push go past him at the slant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:45:*Inward*, and the drop, right, under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:49:*Nothing*, and he stood, and the strike came instead of the push, and he met it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:87:He knew why, too. He had known why since he was nine years old, sitting on an upturned crate in Hesk's workshop with a cup of milk, while Hesk fitted a bearing into a housing on the bench. A man had come round that morning selling something on credit, nothing to pay until spring, and Hesk had sent him away. Cael had asked why, because it had seemed like a good thing to be given something and pay nothing. Hesk had gone on easing the bearing in for a while without answering. *Lad,* he had said at last, *anything that charges you nothing at the counter means to send the bill later. And it'll send it in a coin you never said you'd pay in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:105:He tried it standing closer, and then farther off. He tried it with Brom not striking at all, only gathering. He tried it with his eyes half shut, the way he had been standing when it came. Then, last, he tried knocking first, from a planted foot, to see whether the knock would lead him to it. The knock came back *inward*, clean and ordinary, and he redirected the push at the slant out of pure habit before he could stop himself. The shoulder sent its bill at once, all along the groove, as if to show him what a real price felt like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:135:"No." Cael was quite sure. He had the Log by heart; he could have recited every field of every entry. *Wind-adjacent. Pressure-adjacent. Iron-adjacent.* Three, and their notices, and their costs. "There's nothing in the Log like it. Nothing I've had has ever done that, or anything near it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:149:Cael found that he was grateful for that, more grateful than he could have said. Brom had always kept the two things apart: what he had felt, and what he would claim. That was the same line the Log had been ruled down from its first page, *claim* on one side and *evidence* on the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:189:She listened to all of it without a word. When he came to Brom's hands resting on his chest, with nothing behind them, she put her own hand flat on the staff. When he came to *Tide-adjacent* she looked up, and did not look away again until he had finished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:205:He did not put it under any of the three entries, because it did not belong to any of them. He turned instead to the back of the book, past the two columns and the *Carrying* page, to a clean leaf, and ruled a line across the top of it and wrote a heading he had never needed before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:235:She did not mention it once in the whole hour. She came at him from the right and the left and the front with nothing before it, and he knocked when he could and dropped when he could not, and called what the knock gave him, and she said *yes* or *no* to each. At the end of it she stood in the middle of the alcove with her breath going white and looked at him for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:31:"It might. It went because I sent it." Dace let that stand; he did not seem to want it taken off him. "He said to tell you something. I'll give it you the way he said it, near enough. He said he's not here to embarrass you, or anybody. He came because yours was the first story in three years worth the road. And then he said, *I'd like it to stay that kind of fight.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:59:Feryn, the Bronze from his first year whose Pressure he carried now, a rank two of the long kind who built his weight so slowly you could watch it come. Darrow Innes, Bronze, rank one, the win in the Cinder House yard that had put his name in Vell's book. Sarel, Bronze, rank one, the worst loss he had ever had, underlined twice. On paper, Iron rank eight sat a step below every one of them, under the line where the guilds began to write *Bronze*, and a clerk with a ledger would have said that Cael had beaten worse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:63:And the rank was not the worst of it. He turned to the back of the notebook, to his own method in its first form, the bench and the hooks and the three layers and the four months he had watched Keth, the Blade whose seam had won him his rating. Every fight he had ever won above his weight he had won on a bench first, watching; that was what the grey book was for, and what *he* was for, if he was honest. This time there was nothing to watch and never would be. No bout of the man's had been fought within three hundred miles, and if some carter had seen one in some town on the road it would not have mattered, because the man threw away whatever had been seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:105:He went up for bread a little after midday with the dock partner's bruise still warm on his shoulder, and the whole row seemed to have got there before him. The pie woman, who had said *Cael* to him every day for three weeks, said it again, and then added nothing, which was new. Two carters at the pump stopped talking when he passed. That was not new; but this time one of them called after him, quite cheerfully, "Up the river, is it?" and the other hit him on the arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:119:It went on like that the whole length of the row, though nobody said the visitor's name to him, or needed to. The chestnut man gave him two chestnuts for the price of one and would not take the other copper. The mending-stall woman looked at him over her iron spectacles as he passed, and said *lad* in a voice that meant several things at once, one of which was plainly *I hope you know what you're doing*. A boy he did not know ran past him on the market steps and shouted *up the river!* as if it were a game, and was gone before Cael could see his face. By the time he reached the baker's door he understood that the district had decided something about the fight before he had decided anything about it himself, and that what it had decided was that the fight was partly theirs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:Every square had an *L* in the top corner and another *L* at the foot, small, for after supper. Most had a *B* in the middle, with a question mark beside it. The last square was empty except for a short line drawn across it, like the mark Bede had used for a name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:149:Lira listened with her chin on her fist. Then she took up the charcoal again and wrote, in the square for the morning after next, very small, *right foot*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:155:"The dock man told me about your heels. You've told me now as well, and you told me on the first day, which is new." She looked at him across the pot with something that was not quite a smile. "I'll put that on the bill too, if you like. *Told me the first day.* I'll frame it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:165:"You sat down so suddenly the post swung." She wrote a small *E* in every square along the bottom row, one after another, with great firmness. "There. Now it's on the bill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:195:Lira picked up the charcoal and rubbed out the question marks beside every *B*, one by one, with her thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:200:The hesitation was already in the book, under its heading, where he had put it before supper. He turned past it to the two columns, where the question mark was still there in the margin, in pencil, beside *The right is only ever my feet*. He looked at it for a long time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:204:He did not move the line. Not yet. But under the question mark he wrote, small, *L. has the right foot. Friday.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:206:Then he turned to a clean page and wrote the man's name at the top of it, the only name he had, and the tier and the Path and the age. And under it he wrote everything he knew, which took four lines, because that was all there was. *Paid for since ten; academy at eleven; never the same way twice; asks for that kind of fight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-46.md:33:He had not decided to; it was the old reflex, the one that had kept him whole since the Cinder House: a blow coming from the left and too close, and the hip dropping and loosening and taking him front-left round it, half a body, clean. He felt the burst go and was glad of it for the length of a breath. Then he was in the lock, two *ands* of bright stillness at the end of the burst, standing exactly where it had put him; and Lira was already there. She had not followed the burst. She had stepped once, without hurry, to the place the burst always ended, and the end of her staff was resting on his collarbone before the second *and* was out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-46.md:55:He sat on a while longer with his ribs and forearms singing and thought about what she had said, *be moving when it comes*, and about Maud standing in the middle of her own footprints while Lira went round and round her. It was strange to be taught a thing by somebody who had only learned it a fortnight ago, on the wrong end of it, with the backs of her hands. It was stranger still to see that she was giving it to him before she had properly finished learning it herself, while it still hurt. He did not know whether that was generous or only Lira, and he suspected that with her there was not much difference.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-46.md:99:It was quiet work, and it was slow, and nobody watching from the arch would have seen anything at all. Brom stood and Cael stood, a forearm apart, and every little while Brom's weight gathered, deep in him, the way it gathered before he hardened and sent, and Cael knocked from his planted foot at the moment he thought the meaning came, and felt for what came back. Sometimes it came back clean: *there, and going forward, toward me*. Sometimes it came back as smear, a sense of somebody large and nothing more, like a word heard through a wall. And sometimes Brom meant nothing and sent nothing, and the knock came back with only the weight of a man standing, and Cael had to say *nothing* and be sure of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-46.md:103:It was not like looking, and it never had been, which was why it was so hard to teach. Looking went out from him along a line, and came back with whatever was at the end of the line. The knock did not go anywhere; it was more like rapping once on a door he was already standing against and feeling, through his own knuckles and his own skin, which way the house on the other side was leaning. When it answered clean, the answer arrived all at once and without words, and he had to put the words to it afterward: *there; heavy; going forward*; when it smeared, there were no words to put. And every knock, answered or not, cost him a little behind the eyes, a thin stripe of ache that came a moment after and did not quite leave before the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-46.md:212:"It's you said by somebody who paid for it." She handed the Log back. "You'd have got there. It would've taken you four pages and you'd have used the word *architecture*. He did it in four words because he lay on a floor in front of his whole house and had three years to think about it." She went to her own door. "Use it. It doesn't matter where it came from. Good advice doesn't care who bled for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-46.md:214:Her door closed. He sat and looked at the line, and then, under it, wrote what she had said about meeting it moving, and the half-beat, and *right foot, tomorrow*. Then he wrote one more line, and looked at it, and did not cross it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:15:It was worse than defending. Defending had pulled his attention to the blows; this pulled his attention to nothing, because there was nothing in him to pull. The knock went out thin and came back thinner. Twice he planted and arrived and simply forgot to ask, and stood there with the gap going by while Brom gathered and sent. Once he knocked and got *inward* clean and could not make his legs do anything about it, and the push took him in the chest and sat him down on the stone, and he sat there a moment looking at the beams before he got up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:49:*About one in five fails,* he wrote, under the columns. *At worst. At a forearm good; at a pace still fair; at two paces still nothing. It answers use. It deepens where it's worked. Brom built the steps; I only climbed them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:77:The betting man had given up on his slab altogether and set up a board on two trestles by the pump. It had two columns on it now, side by side, and the columns did not agree. Down the left, under *practitioners*, the man from up the river was a long way in front, the way a man with a guild card for Iron rank eight would be in front in any room where people understood what the card meant. Down the right, under *the row*, it was nearly even, and every time the betting man shortened the left, somebody from the stalls came and laid a copper on the right and he had to shorten that as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:115:He did it lightly, as he would have on any man on any bench he meant to learn, because not doing it would have been a lie about who he was. He went in at the first layer, the outward one, *what does he do*, and began to look for the things a body gave off when it sat still. A shifting of weight from one hip to the other. The small endless fidgets of a hand that had been trained to hold something. The way a Path sat in a man when he was not using it, which nearly always showed somewhere, in a set of the jaw or a tightness across the back or a breath held a fraction too long. A year of benches had taught him that stillness always leaked. A man sitting doing nothing told you more about himself in ten minutes than he would in a bout, because in a bout he was thinking about you, and on a bench he had forgotten anybody was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:123:*He knows I'm looking,* Cael thought. *He knows what a bench can take off a resting body, and he's taking care of it himself. He's not leaking because he's decided not to.* He had never sat across a room from anybody who could do that. He had only ever thought of it as a thing he hoped one day to be able to do himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:161:And that was all Reydan could say. He could judge what he saw, and he had judged it: a boy who watched like a master and had been worked hard by people who cared whether he lived. What the boy was, under that, he did not know. The keeper's line said *no Path designation*. The boy's body, sitting, said nothing about a Path at all, which might mean anything. Reydan sat on the end of the west bench and found, for the second time on that hill, that he did not want to guess.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:183:He wrote it in the grey book on his knee, under no mark at all, because he did not have a mark for her and did not want one yet. *The woman with the book. Academy pass tonight; Dace honoured it; whose, it doesn't say. Writes when I do something new. Won't be looked at back.* He looked at it. Then, smaller: *Tomorrow is a bout. It may turn into something else before it's finished. I don't know what.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:215:The lamp burned down a little as he sat. Out in the main room the sweepers went over the stone, and the eel-market men's handcart went out at the side door with its pots knocking, and voices went past the arch saying *tomorrow*, and went on. He turned the pages back, past the fortnight's tallies and the clerk's account and Ansel's four words, and past the two columns, with *Going right* crossed out of *Shape* and written into *Habit* with its cost. He went past the three entries with their notices at the head, Wind and Pressure and Iron, and further, to the eight unasked instances carried from the old book, and the farmer, and *copies of real people, taken on real days*. A year and more of it. He had come down the hill with a bag and a direction, and this was what he had to show for it, and it was not nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:223:*Wind: three an exchange at most, and the lock is two *ands* he'll count. The hip only when I know; when I don't, the feet, which have no doorway.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:237:He turned to the back again, to the page headed *Anomalies*, and read the entry through. Under it there was a space he had left without knowing why. He knew why now. He wrote one line in it, in the field he had always kept at the foot of every entry for whatever did not fit anywhere else.

--- trailing whitespace ---
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-50.md:179:"That's true," said Brom, without any vanity at all, "but it's still not much." 

