## (1) Brief items

### 1. Havel's comparison files — RESOLVED

The `-U0` diff removes both false claims that Havel personally handled five earlier `[SHATTERED]` files. In ch14 it replaces “He had handled five files of this classification in four years” with a canon-based archive comparison:

> “There had been four of them in the registry's whole history. The instructors had sent his year down to the archive's reading room to read all four...”

> “Not one of them had been open for more than a few weeks before it was closed, and every one was as thick as a ledger...”

The comparison with Cael's file remains functional and is now logically explicit: “This one had been open for more than a year. It closed easily. It was as thin as a market permit”; “a file did not stay thin for a year by itself.”

In ch15 the diff replaces “He had handled five files of this class” and the five-file marker inference with two properly bounded observations:

> “Passive monitoring was a district officer's work, and in four years he had done it on files of every class that called for it. Not one of them had ever carried a marker from above a district officer's grade.”

> “The four old files of this class in the archive had carried markers enough, but every one of them signed, dated and explained...”

This preserves Havel's ability to recognize the unsigned marker as procedurally impossible without implying five surviving historical subjects or five earlier cases in his own caseload.

#### Specific continuity ruling: PASS; no fix

“All four ... none open more than a few weeks” is consistent with “the fourth unknown.” The statements address different facts:

- Book 1 ch4's public historical summary says three practitioners are recorded dead within weeks and the fourth practitioner's **disposition** is unknown; ch6 restates “three ... ended in a few weeks and one in a line that said nobody knew.”
- A registry **file** can be closed without the public summary recording its subject as dead. Later canon makes that distinction explicit. Book 3 ch17 says Cael “read all four files in full,” that removal was complete within days in every case, that “Three of the four files simply ended,” and that “The fourth ended with a single line of closure notation.” The fourth file therefore closed even though Book 1's public disposition remains unknown.
- The universe bible's secret truth—that all four were eliminated within weeks—also fits both surface records. The repaired passage does not reveal that secret; it says only that the files closed.

The access premise also passes. Book 1 lets fourteen-year-old Cael enter the historical classification archive and read the full summary. Book 3 lets Cael read all four enforcement files in full with archive access. No later-book canon says these files are restricted to senior officers or forbids a registry training year from reading them under instruction. Havel's supervised reading-room access is therefore consistent.

“Every one ... signed, dated and explained” does not conflict with Book 3's closure account. It describes the markers in the archived files, not the completeness or honesty of the public disposition entries. It is also consonant with Book 3's description of those cases as documented with the enforcement provisions' usual thoroughness.

No exact fix is needed.

